\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Officials At Fed Take Jobs Data In Stride\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Timiraos \par \uc2 552 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 July's jobs report rattled financial markets and meaningfully raised the prospect that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates at its next meeting in September.\par \par \uc2 But two Fed officials on Friday maintained that the U.S. labor market is still well balanced.\par \par \uc2 "What we're seeing I would describe over the past year as a gentle gradual cooling of the labor market, but still leaving it in a still solid place," \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  President \uc2 John Williams\uc2  said in an interview. An ally of Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , he said he would go into the September meeting with "very much an open mind" about cutting interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Fed officials split over their decision to hold rates steady at their meeting on Wednesday, with two voting to cut. Nine others supported the decision to hold steady.\par \par \uc2 Even among those who favored taking no action in July, a divide is emerging over whether to worry more about the labor market or inflation, a challenge for Powell in forging consensus.\par \par \uc2 While the unemployment rate rose only to 4.2% from 4.1%, soggier payroll figures give Powell room to build agreement for a rate decrease because they undercut a narrative that the labor market has strong momentum. Officials will see one more month of job data before their next meeting.\par \par \uc2 Unusually large downward revisions to job gains in May and June had been "really the news of this report," said Williams. "This is important information . . . to understand the direction of what we're seeing in supply and demand for labor" and the labor market's cooler momentum.\par \par \uc2 A wider range of indicators including job openings and initial claims for jobless benefits paint a picture of an economy that remains in the same slow-to-hire, slow-to-fire equilibrium that has been a feature for the past year, Williams said. "The labor market still is solid in my view," he said.\par \par \uc2 Cleveland Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Beth Hammack\uc2  echoed that assessment. In an interview Friday with \uc2 Bloomberg Television\uc2 , she said she sees "a healthy labor market that's still well in balance, but with some disappointing signs that we should watch very carefully." She added that weaker job growth makes sense given reduced immigration.\par \par \uc2 White House economic adviser Stephen Miran said Friday's jobs report was "a disappointment" but attributed much of the weakness to technical and temporary factors.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, he said about 60% of the downward revisions to May and June job gains were due to seasonal adjustment issues. Policy uncertainty around tariffs had weighed on hiring, but its resolution with recently announced trade deals should allow job growth to rebound in coming months, he said.\par \par \uc2 Fed governors \uc2 Michelle Bowman\uc2  and \uc2 Christopher Waller\uc2 , who dissented in favor of a rate cut at Wednesday's meeting, issued statements before Friday's report warning of weaker labor-market conditions if the Fed didn't resume the rate cuts begun last year.\par \par \uc2 Williams said it was natural to have divided views given elevated uncertainty. His approach reflects his view that the Fed still has work to do on inflation. He noted that a widely watched gauge that excludes volatile food and energy prices is "still meaningfully above" the Fed's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250802el820002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
