\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Work for \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 ? Not Even for $1 Billion. --- Loyalties and big personalities rule in talent wars\b0\par\par\uc2 By Berber Jin and Keach Hagey \par \uc2 1,272 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  sought to play catch-up in the generative AI race, he reached out a few months ago to \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's former chief technology officer, \uc2 Mira Murati\uc2 , and offered to buy her fledgling startup, \uc2 Thinking Machines Lab\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When she said no, the \uc2 Meta\uc2  chief executive responded by launching a full-scale raid. In the following weeks he approached more than a dozen of Murati's roughly 50 employees to sound them out about jumping ship. His chief target: Andrew Tulloch, a leading researcher and co-founder at the startup.\par \par \uc2 To peel him off, Zuckerberg dangled a billion-dollar package that could, with top bonuses and extraordinary stock performance, have been worth as much as $1.5 billion over at least six years, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Tulloch said no. None of his colleagues left either.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesman Andy Stone called the description of the offer "inaccurate and ridiculous" and said that any compensation package is predicated on a stock rising. He added that \uc2 Meta\uc2  is not interested in acquiring \uc2 Thinking Machines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even in Silicon Valley, where star engineers have long wielded outsize economic power, turning down nine-figure pay packages is rare. But as the battle royal for AI talent escalates, the companies with the biggest war chests are finding the cash cannon only gets them so far.\par \par \uc2 While some AI researchers act like free agents, bouncing between labs in pursuit of more pay and power, quite a few display an unwavering allegiance to their chosen leaders, larger-than-life figures who, in the tech industry, carry the single-name cachet of rock stars. The idiosyncratic cultures of the different startups bind employees to one another. Meanwhile, after years of poaching en ronde, the companies involved are getting savvier about playing defense.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and startups launched by its alumni like Murati are the frequent targets of Zuckerberg's recruiting blitz. From the earliest days of the AI race, pioneers lured researchers with the promise of getting to work for the historic mission of creating artificial general intelligence, or a system that is smarter than a human at most tasks. It was \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , whose co-founders include \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2 , \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and Ilya Sutskever, that turned that mission into a quasi-religious quest, with its nonprofit charter to ensure that the work benefits humanity.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  has reached out to more than 100 of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's employees. It has hired at least 10. On July 25, Zuckerberg picked Shengjia Zhao, a Chinese researcher who spent three years at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , to lead \uc2 Meta\uc2 's new superintelligence team.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  researchers who have so far rebuffed \uc2 Meta\uc2 's advances chose to remain because they believed \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  was the closest to reaching artificial general intelligence, wanted to work at a smaller company and were wary of having the fruits of their labors go toward a product that was primarily driven by advertising, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg has recruited even fewer researchers from \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 , the $170 billion startup led by \uc2 Dario Amodei\uc2 , who took some of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's top talent with him when he left five years ago to start it.\par \par \uc2 All of \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's seven co-founders are still at the company. Many of them met Amodei through the tightknit world of effective altruism over a decade ago, a social movement that grew popular among researchers for its early concerns that AI could go rogue and destroy humanity. Some of them lived in a group house in San Francisco, debating the most effective way to give away their wealth and the risk that AI posed.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg has hired at least two employees from the startup -- Joel Pobar and Anton Bakhtin -- both of whom previously spent multiple years working at \uc2 Meta\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sutskever has built the startup he co-founded last year, \uc2 Safe Superintelligence\uc2 , or SSI, in a way that makes it relatively poach-proof. Unlike Amodei, Sutskever didn't pick off a large group of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  researchers to join him.\par \par \uc2 Most of Sutskever's staffers aren't well-known in Silicon Valley, in part because the company is looking for promising technologists with new ideas whom Sutskever can mentor. They are discouraged from mentioning SSI on their \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profiles, in part to prevent other companies from trying to snatch them away. Earlier this year, Sutskever rebuffed an offer from Zuckerberg to buy SSI.\par \par \uc2 Murati, who spent six years at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  before leaving last September, has her own band of apostles. Originally from Albania, she joined \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  when it was a small research lab, helped launch its first product and came to run almost every aspect of the company as CTO.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , she was known for her emotional intelligence and lack of ego, which earned her the loyalty of the research and engineering staff. At \uc2 Thinking Machines\uc2 , she has instituted a similarly egoless reporting hierarchy as \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and SSI, where even senior researchers are listed as a "Member of Technical Staff" in a nod to the flat, collegial culture of Bell Labs, one of the inspirations behind \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When she started \uc2 Thinking Machines\uc2  in February, more than 20 \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  colleagues followed her, including co-founder John Schulman, who had decamped to \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  just a few months prior. Many of Murati's researchers came from \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's post-training team, the research division that built ChatGPT and was in charge of teaching AI models how to communicate with humans.\par \par \uc2 What exactly Murati is building remains a closely guarded secret even to some of her investors, who recently poured $2 billion into the company. Its stated mission is making "AI systems more widely understood, customizable and generally capable." Murati recently said the company was "building multimodal AI that works with how you naturally interact with the world," and would share its first product in the "next couple months." The startup has leased an office in one of the quieter parts of San Francisco's Mission District, just a couple blocks away from \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Wired has previously reported some of the details of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's outreach to \uc2 Thinking Machines\uc2 ' talent.\par \par \uc2 Tulloch, listed as a co-founder at \uc2 Thinking Machines\uc2 , was the subject of a spirited recruitment campaign from Zuckerberg and \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2 , the newly appointed head of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's superintelligence lab, both of whom peppered him with messages asking him to join.\par \par \uc2 Originally from Australia, Tulloch graduated from the \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2 , where he had the highest GPA among science students. He worked on machine learning at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  for 18 months before starting graduate school at Cambridge. He then moved to California to work at \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's AI Research Group, eventually becoming a distinguished engineer, one of the highest technical roles at the company. "He was definitely known as an extreme genius," said \uc2 Mike Vernal\uc2 , a former \uc2 Facebook\uc2  executive who overlapped with Tulloch.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, a few years after Tulloch joined \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's president \uc2 Greg Brockman\uc2  tried to hire him as one of the organization's first employees, writing in an email to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  that he was getting $800,000 a year at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and would likely try to negotiate up. At the time, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  was offering new recruits a $175,000 annual salary with a $125,000 annual bonus.\par \par \uc2 "Andrew is very close to saying yes. However, he's concerned about taking such a large paycut," Brockman wrote on Feb. 21.\par \par \uc2 He didn't join \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  then.\par \par \uc2 Tulloch eventually came aboard seven years later, when ChatGPT was already a viral sensation and the startup's valuation soared.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250802el820001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
