\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: U.S. Watch\b0\par\par\uc2 529 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\par \par \uc2 Maxwell Seeks\par \par \uc2 Testimony Immunity\par \par \uc2 Ghislaine Maxwell won't answer lawmakers' questions about her dealings with convicted sex offender \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  unless Congress meets several conditions or President Trump grants her clemency, her lawyer said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to the House Oversight Committee, Maxwell's lawyer David Markus said she would appear only after resolving her appeal of her sex-trafficking conviction before the Supreme Court. Markus added that Maxwell would require immunity for any questioning, saying she couldn't risk further criminal exposure, and would need a list of questions in advance.\par \par \uc2 Maxwell was responding to a subpoena for her testimony from the committee, chaired by Rep. James Comer (R., Ky.). Without the requested assurances, Markus said in the letter, Maxwell would invoke her Fifth Amendment right against self-incrimination. "Of course, in the alternative, if Ms. Maxwell were to receive clemency, she would be willing -- and eager -- to testify openly and honestly," Markus told Comer.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the committee said it "will respond to Ms. Maxwell's attorney soon, but it will not consider granting congressional immunity for her testimony."\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has come under sustained criticism after releasing a memo saying that it didn't intend to make public any further information from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation into Epstein and his crimes. Figures on both the right and left accused them of backtracking on earlier promises to do so, while the administration said it had already released all of the pertinent material that it could, following an exhaustive review.\par \par \uc2 -- Sadie Gurman and Olivia Beavers\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 LABOR MARKET\par \par \uc2 Hiring Is Cooling,\par \par \uc2 Survey Suggests\par \par \uc2 Hiring and job opportunities declined last month in the U.S., a Labor Department survey showed, suggesting the labor market turned even less friendly to people who are out of work.\par \par \uc2 In June, 5.2 million Americans were hired into new jobs, down from 5.5 million in May, and the hiring rate declined to 3.3% from 3.4% a month earlier, according to the monthly Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey, or Jolts.\par \par \uc2 The number of open jobs fell to 7.4 million from 7.7 million in May, and the job-openings rate declined 0.2 percentage point to 4.4%. The survey reflected another month of a labor market that is losing some dynamism even as it avoids large-scale job losses. Many businesses have been slow to lay off people, but they also are pausing hiring plans, yielding a tougher road for people seeking work.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 TRADE DEFICIT\par \par \uc2 U.S. Trade Deficit\par \par \uc2 for Goods Shrinks\par \par \uc2 Fewer goods traded both ways across U.S. borders last month, but the deficit fell as imports dropped more than exports.\par \par \uc2 June's goods-trade deficit stood at $86 billion, according to preliminary numbers from the Commerce Department. That was down 11% from $96.4 billion in May.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration's tariff swings have disrupted trade patterns this year. At the start of 2025, the deficit spiked to record highs as companies rushed to buy overseas goods before expected import taxes snapped into place. It then shrank in April after steep new tariffs discouraged imports.\par \par \uc2 -- Matt Grossman\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250730el7u00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
