\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Verizon\uc2  Tightens Work-in-Office Policy --- Most managers must report three days a week; company sets headquarters move\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate King and Patience Haggin \par \uc2 526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Telecommunications giant \uc2 Verizon Communications\uc2  is moving its headquarters to a larger office space in Manhattan as it ramps up in-office requirements for most of its management employees.\par \par \uc2 Verizon\uc2  has leased 200,000 square feet in a recently renovated office tower near Penn Station, according to people familiar with the matter. \uc2 Verizon\uc2  plans to consolidate next year over 1,000 employees currently working in two other Manhattan locations.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the move, \uc2 Verizon\uc2  is tightening up its return-to-office policy. Management and corporate employees approved for hybrid work will be required to report to the office three days a week, beginning the day after Labor Day, said a company spokesman.\par \par \uc2 Previously, the company required these employees to work from the office eight days a month. Employees approved for full-time remote work will likely not be affected. "We believe that our teams perform best when we are together," the \uc2 Verizon\uc2  spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Numerous companies have been requiring workers to spend more time in the office. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  this month required corporate workers to come to the office a minimum of four days a week, up from three. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  have also toughened their requirements.\par \par \uc2 The new mandates have been good news for the New York City office market, which has been rebounding from one of the worst office downturns since World War II. Business districts in New York, along with the rest of the country, have suffered weak demand ever since they were emptied out by the pandemic more than five years ago.\par \par \uc2 But in the past year, companies have become more demanding in requiring workers to be in offices. Financial services companies, a major driver of New York's economy, have been among those leading the return-to-office charge.\par \par \uc2 Office tenants have leased more than 20 million square feet in Manhattan so far in 2025, the strongest first-half-year period in more than a decade, according to Frank Wallach, a Colliers executive managing director of research. Manhattan's availability rate fell in the second quarter to 15.4%, its lowest level since January 2021.\par \par \uc2 Verizon\uc2 's new headquarters will be in Penn 2, a recently renovated building owned by office giant \uc2 Vornado Realty Trust\uc2 . \uc2 Vornado\uc2  spent $1.2 billion overhauling Penn 2 and a nearby office tower during the depths of the pandemic. The 1.8 million-square-foot Penn 2 includes a rooftop park and pavilion, auditorium space that can seat 300 and several cafes.\par \par \uc2 Penn 2 also houses the corporate headquarters for the nearby \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  sports and entertainment complex as well as Major League Soccer. Earlier this spring, \uc2 Universal Music Group\uc2 , home to Def Jam Recordings and other labels, signed a 22-year lease for four office floors and a ground-floor retail location.\par \par \uc2 Verizon\uc2  plans to focus on three core campuses going forward -- its global headquarters in New York City, its operational headquarters in Basking Ridge, N.J., and a campus in Irving, Texas, according to a person familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250729el7t0001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
