\par\par \b \uc2 Intel Sets Layoffs For 15% Of Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robbie Whelan \par \uc2 682 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Intel detailed dramatic steps to revive its sagging fortunes, outlining layoffs of 15% of its workforce and scrapping plans to spend tens of billions of dollars on new chip facilities in Europe.\par \par \uc2 The chip-making giant said Thursday it would refocus its strategy on the highly competitive market for AI chips, regaining market share in personal-computer processors and developing its advanced 14A technology to sell to large customers.\par \par \uc2 Intel, which has long dominated the business of making chips for laptops and desktop computers, fell far behind competitors such as \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices\uc2  and \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing\uc2  after it failed to anticipate the surge in demand for the powerful chips fueling the artificial-intelligence boom.\par \par \uc2 "There are no more blank checks," Intel Chief Executive \uc2 Lip-Bu Tan\uc2  wrote in a memo to staff. "Every investment must make economic sense."\par \par \uc2 Revenue in the June quarter was roughly flat at $12.9 billion, beating Wall Street expectations, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Intel's quarterly loss widened to $2.9 billion for the quarter from a $1.6 billion loss in the year-earlier period. The results represent the company's sixth-consecutive quarterly loss, extending its longest streak in 35 years.\par \par \uc2 Sales in Intel's PC chip division -- the company's largest segment -- fell 3% from the year-earlier period to $7.9 billion but beat analyst estimates. Finance chief \uc2 Dave Zinsner\uc2  said the company saw some benefit from President Trump's import tariff announcements. Major customers rushed to buy chips for laptops and desktop PCs ahead of the levies taking effect, but the effect wasn't as much as expected, he said.\par \par \uc2 Revenue rose 4% to $3.9 billion in the division that sells chips for data centers and AI. Revenue rose 3% to $4.4 billion in its contract chip-making business, but the division saw its loss widen to $3.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Tan, who took the helm at Intel in March, faces a herculean task in turning the company around and is under pressure to quickly provide investors with a road map for how he plans to solve a complex web of challenges.\par \par \uc2 Intel became a household name by developing a string of evermore powerful and efficient processors for laptops and PCs just as American households grew obsessed with buying and upgrading their home computers. But the company fell behind a new crop of competitors like \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , AMD and \uc2 TSMC\uc2  after it failed to anticipate the boom in demand for the powerful chips used to develop AI models.\par \par \uc2 Tan, a veteran semiconductor industry investor, replaced previous CEO \uc2 Pat Gelsinger\uc2 , with whom he had clashed during a previous stint as a company board member.\par \par \uc2 Gelsinger oversaw an enormous expansion in Intel's factory footprint, betting that Intel's future lay in contract manufacturing of chips for outside customers. This effort was helped by President Joe Biden's 2022 Chips Act, which provided billions in government funding for the expansion. Intel must meet construction and operational milestones in order to receive the money.\par \par \uc2 Zinsner, the finance chief, said that Intel's 18A technology, which is used mostly to make PC chips designed by Intel, is "the most important thing we're doing right now." The company plans to launch its Panther Lake line of mobile processors, the first product to be made using 18A, later this year.\par \par \uc2 Looking forward, it is the company's 14A technology, a process that allows for more efficient chips with more transistors crammed onto them, that Intel hopes will help drive growth and attract large chip designers as customers. Intel is hoping to develop the technology by the end of the decade so it can better compete with other big contract manufacturers like \uc2 TSMC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As for AI, Tan's Thursday memo said the company plans to concentrate on products related to the development of models capable of decision-making and other complex tasks.\par \par \uc2 Intel forecast a range of $12.6 to $13.6 billion in revenue for the current quarter, higher than estimates by analysts polled by \uc2 FactSet\uc2 .\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich446\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6690
