\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Liberation Day for American AI\b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy has proven resilient amid the Biden regulatory onslaught and President Trump's tariff whipsaw, and one reason is investment in artificial intelligence. Credit the Trump team for trying to keep America's latest golden goose alive.\par \par \uc2 The White House on Wednesday proposed a hodgepodge of policies to ensure that China doesn't steal an AI march. The goal is to liberate innovators from burdensome regulation, bulldoze impediments to new data centers, and "hyper-scale" private investment, to borrow an industry buzzword.\par \par \uc2 Competition and investment are already enormous. \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  plans to spend some $75 billion this year. Meta is investing between $64 billion and $72 billion and poaching engineers from competitors with baseball-star-level pay packages. \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is working with SoftBank and others on a $500 billion project to build data centers in the next four years.\par \par \uc2 Biden antitrust cops sought to chill this AI competition with whale-hunting expeditions into \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . The Trump report recommends the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  drop investigations begun under Biden Chair Lina Khan that "advance theories of liability that unduly burden AI innovation" and to modify agency orders that do.\par \par \uc2 The report also recommends that federal agencies establish exclusions under the National Environmental Policy Act for data centers. This will reduce the permitting rigmarole that can delay projects, though legislation will be needed to prevent endless litigation. How about an action plan for Congress?\par \par \uc2 A paucity of electric power limits the buildout of new data centers. The president of the independent watchdog for \uc2 PJM Interconnection\uc2 , the nation's largest grid operator and a hub for new data centers, warned this week that "there is simply no new capacity to meet new loads" from AI hyper-scalers.\par \par \uc2 Biden Environmental Protection Agency rules slowed AI by forcing the premature shutdown of coal plants and making it inordinately expensive to build new natural gas plants. Mr. Trump is rolling back these rules, which will prevent power shortages from getting worse.\par \par \uc2 Still, the U.S. desperately needs more reliable baseload power to support AI growth. Renewable subsidies and state mandates have distorted financial incentives in energy markets. The result is excessive solar and wind power, which has undercut baseload gas, coal and nuclear plants in wholesale markets.\par \par \uc2 The report recommends reforming "power markets to align financial incentives with the goal of grid stability." That means changing wholesale market rules to ensure baseload plants are adequately compensated for providing reliable electricity around the clock. This would encourage the construction of more gas plants and counter subsidy distortion.\par \par \uc2 America will also need hundreds of thousands of workers to train, build and run AI data centers. Data center workers are in such demand that they can earn $200,000 a year. The report usefully recommends reforming government-registered apprenticeships and worker training programs, which are ineffective and a bureaucratic nightmare to navigate.\par \par \uc2 Alas, the Administration's AI ambitions run headlong into its restrictionist immigration policies. The U.S. will need many more foreign workers to train AI models and build data centers. Restricting human capital won't help the U.S. dominate AI.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250724el7o0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
