\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: AI-Talent Frenzy Is Mixed Bag for Venture Capitalists\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yuliya Chernova \par \uc2 753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Big checks. Fast paydays. What's not to love for investors in companies whose founders are recruitment targets in the AI-talent game? Turns out many investors are wary.\par \par \uc2 Investors are getting nervous that founders of their portfolio companies could become targets of stupendous recruitment offers in the competition for talent in artificial intelligence. Even if shareholders end up making money, it is sometimes less than they bargained for, or the exit comes earlier than expected. In short, the deals might be more lucrative for the founders than the shareholders. Some of the most-promising startups, some investors fear, are being defanged before they reach what investors believe is their full potential.\par \par \uc2 "We funded the guy's dream and somebody else convinced him to give up on that dream," Salil Deshpande, general partner and founder of venture firm Uncorrelated Ventures.\par \par \uc2 "We invest in people at the early stages, we expect them to create value in the company we are investing in," he said. "If someone will pay them enough to abandon what they are working on, that's a risk. It's a big risk. I'm not happy about that."\par \par \uc2 On the bright side, some recent deals that accompanied big hires allowed shareholders to generate strong returns.\par \par \uc2 This month, \uc2 Google\uc2  hired the founders of coding startup Windsurf, as well as part of its team and cashed out the startup's shareholders for a total of $2.4 billion. A venture firm that first backed Windsurf in its seed round returned about 50 times invested capital, according to a person familiar with the situation. Another VC that came in at the Series B into Windsurf got a return of four times investment, according to another person.\par \par \uc2 Even some venture firms are subject to early wind-downs as a result of AI recruitment drives. When \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  hired Daniel Gross and \uc2 Nat Friedman\uc2 , co-founders of venture firm NFDG, to join the company's Superintelligence Labs, they decided their venture firm wouldn't raise additional funds. Gross stepped down from his role as CEO of venture-backed startup \uc2 Safe Superintelligence\uc2 , which was valued at $30 billion in a funding round earlier this year. Limited partners in NFDG were offered cash from the first fund at a markup.\par \par \uc2 When institutions back new venture firms, they hope to stick with the same managers, if they are successful, through many funds, not just one. That is because venture investors who create high returns for LPs are very rare, and if you find them, you don't want them to give up investing.\par \par \uc2 Recent tech hires have generated early and quick returns for LPs and VCs, in some cases creating high internal rates of return, a metric that measures returns over the investment's life. IRR is higher if the exit happens faster. Yet that isn't necessarily best for investors in the long run.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe you thought it was going to be a huge winner, and while IRR feels good, the reality is you need a high multiple to have a great fund," said Matt Murphy, partner at \uc2 Menlo Ventures\uc2 . A quick exit at a lower absolute dollar amount, that is, potentially removes the fund's top asset.\par \par \uc2 Deshpande said there is an upside to an early successful exit -- it is cash now versus uncertainty. A startup could have become an even bigger win in the future, or it could fail.\par \par \uc2 High initial valuations for some AI companies are exacerbating the risks for investors and misaligning incentives with founders in acquisitions. In a hypothetical scenario, say a startup raises $1 billion at a $10 billion valuation and gets acquired for $10 billion. Investors would get just their money back while the founders and the rest of the team would get $9 billion, making it a huge win. This math would have been true in the past, but today the rate of multibillion-dollar early valuations in AI, as well as big acquisition offers for top talent, is more frequent.\par \par \uc2 Investors say large compensation offers to founders, such as via retention grants, can make the deals more enticing for founders than for their shareholders.\par \par \uc2 Murphy said the market dynamics around AI talent are making him worried. "We are still early in the AI wave, and so the outcome is asymmetric between founders, employees and investors," he said. The acquirers "are taking the upside off the table by making it extraordinarily financially compelling for founders."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250724el7o0000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
