\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 One Way to Predict Early Career Success\b0\par\par\uc2 By Heidi Mitchell \par \uc2 651 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The strongest predictor of early career success isn't your education, training or the type of experience you bring to the job. Instead, new research suggests one of the most powerful -- and overlooked -- factors is being assigned to a complex, high-profile project as a new hire.\par \par \uc2 Shihan Li, now an assistant professor of management at the University of Kentucky's Gatton College of Business and Economics, worked with \uc2 Carnegie Mellon\uc2  professors David Krackhardt and Nynke M. D. Niezink to analyze data from a high-tech aerospace company in China that tracked almost 500 new engineering hires over two years. The Chinese company rotates every new employee through four six-month projects, each varying in complexity and status, with no regard to the workers' backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 The researchers found that the newcomers randomly placed on complex, high-profile projects during any six-month period during the first two years consistently earned more professional certifications, reported greater learning and received more recognition from the company than peers assigned to less complicated projects. That, in turn, led to higher quarterly bonuses, stronger evaluations and faster promotions.\par \par \uc2 In fact, new hires lucky enough to be assigned to complex projects were, on average, more than five times as likely to be promoted than peers assigned to less-complicated projects, according to Li, who was a graduate student at the time of study.\par \par \uc2 While the researchers looked at just one company, the randomized design -- rare in organizational research -- gave the authors a window into cause and effect.\par \par \uc2 "We were having trouble finding good predictors of job success," Krackhardt says. "What school they went to, what training they had, what kind of experience they brought . . . they all contributed something, but none reliably predicted success," he says. "This is the first time we've been able to systematically link the cause to the earliest assignment itself."\par \par \uc2 The team's findings, Krackhardt says, came from two distinct but complementary mechanisms.\par \par \uc2 First, complex assignments expose new hires to other teams and tricky technical problems, which can accelerate skill development. "When you're in a challenging task, the learning curve is greater," Krackhardt says.\par \par \uc2 Second, status comes with complex projects, which reflects on the new hires. "They were given more opportunities," he says. "They got highlighted in internal newsletters. The status carried with them, even beyond the specific project they were working on."\par \par \uc2 This visibility mattered, especially for employees who didn't come in with premier companies on their resumes. "Being on a high-complexity project is like being on a winning Super Bowl team," Krackhardt says. "Even if you're a backbencher, you still get to wear the Super Bowl ring." And even when the projects didn't fully succeed, the new hires working on them were nonetheless "admired within the firm for their boldness," says Li.\par \par \uc2 While the researchers determined that having previous industry experience wasn't the main predictor of early career success, it did help. "If you were someone with a lot of experience in the field that was useful in the project, you got a bigger bang for the buck," Krackhardt says.\par \par \uc2 In most companies managers get to choose their team members, often favoring high performers or "hotshots," says Krackhardt. That can limit opportunity for new employees who may have potential but aren't self-promoters or well-connected.\par \par \uc2 While random assignment may not be practical everywhere, the principle can still be applied, the researchers say. Organizations can rotate new employees through complex work, open up high-status projects to newbies and give new hires tough assignments earlier, regardless of prior experience or other indicators.\par \par \uc2 As for new hires themselves, Krackhardt encourages them to say yes to hard projects rather than avoiding them, especially if the company's culture supports that sort of courage. "Even one challenging assignment can change your career trajectory," he says.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250724el7o00009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
