\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Democrats Attack Gig-Worker Benefits\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats are breathless about Americans losing government healthcare, but they're dead set against expanding private coverage. They're attacking a plan to help gig workers get benefits -- merely because unions won't get a cut.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana GOP Sen. \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  released several bills last week that would shield companies from a regulatory onslaught if they decide to offer health or retirement plans to their contract workers. Delivery drivers for \uc2 Uber\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and other "gig" platforms would be able to get these benefits without the risk that courts or federal agencies would reclassify them as full employees.\par \par \uc2 The bills would bridge the gap between workers' desire for benefits and their increasing preference for flexible work. As of 2023, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  categorized more than 10% of the U.S. labor force as "contingent workers" -- a category that includes contractors and on-call employees. Surveys show that 75% to 85% of these workers prefer not to be full employees, but progressive regulators have sought to strip independent status from workers who receive any benefits.\par \par \uc2 In a committee hearing on the bill last week, Sen. Bernie Sanders sidestepped the issue of worker benefits to address his party's real concern -- giving unions more power over individual workers. "These bills are about giving corporations the freedom to deny workers the right to form a union," he said.\par \par \uc2 Independent contractors can't unionize under the National Labor Relations Act, so unions and the Democrats they support want to outlaw contract work, or at least deprive it of benefits that could attract workers. Democrats on the committee were united in opposition.\par \par \uc2 This political opposition has deterred several gig companies from offering benefits. \uc2 Uber\uc2  CEO \uc2 Dara Khosrowshahi\uc2  has pledged more than $650 million a year to fund health insurance and paid time off if lawmakers would withdraw the threat of reclassifying the company's drivers.\par \par \uc2 Two Democratic-led states have cracked the door open to contractor benefits. Food-delivery app \uc2 DoorDash\uc2  joined with Pennsylvania and Maryland lawmakers to let the company fund savings accounts for its workers. These pilot programs are time-limited, but their success could draw attention in other states.\par \par \uc2 It's a shame workers will have to wait. If not for the opposition of lawmakers who claim to value healthcare, millions of workers could receive benefits without trading their freedom to get them.\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: Politicians Don't Understand Young Workers" -- WSJ July 29, 2025)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250723el7n0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
