\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Getting Laid Off Later in Life Presents Turbulent Challenges --- People in their 50s and 60s take longer to find new jobs, while others retire earlier than planned\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anne Tergesen and Veronica Dagher \par \uc2 849 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Getting laid off is challenging. Getting laid off later in life is especially so.\par \par \uc2 People in their 50s often have mortgages, college bills and other significant financial responsibilities. And those who lose their jobs can find it harder to get new ones that match their old paychecks.\par \par \uc2 That can undermine retirement security, said Anqi Chen, associate director of savings and household finance at the Center for Retirement Research at Boston College.\par \par \uc2 Some job seekers break into their savings or claim Social Security earlier than planned, resulting in a lower retirement income. Many miss out on making 401(k) or IRA contributions, never mind the catch-up contributions the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  allows for workers 50 and older.\par \par \uc2 While labor-force participation rates for older Americans have risen steadily since the mid-1990s, many in their 50s and 60s face turbulent transitions as they wind down their working lives.\par \par \uc2 These charts show how Americans handle late-career layoffs. (See accompanying illustrations -- WSJ July 22, 2025)\par \par \uc2 It is not uncommon to be laid off late in a career.\par \par \uc2 Those who experience a layoff are more likely than people who quit to try to replace the jobs and salaries they lost.\par \par \uc2 They are less likely to move into a new field than those who quit at similar ages, said Kevin Cahill, an economist at \uc2 FTI Consulting\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Older career workers who find new jobs take an 11% wage cut, on average, according to a forthcoming study Cahill co-wrote. The pay cut was 15% for men and 7% for women.\par \par \uc2 It takes longer for older workers to find a new position, said \uc2 Debra Whitman\uc2 , \uc2 AARP\uc2  chief public policy officer.\par \par \uc2 "Too many employers still wrongly assume older workers are less tech savvy or will retire after a short period on jobs, when many people want or need to work well into their 60s, 70s or 80s," said Whitman.\par \par \uc2 Other obstacles might include higher compensation expectations of the potential employee or the unwillingness of the employer to offer a desirable salary.\par \par \uc2 Another factor, according to Chen, is that there aren't as many senior level positions -- vice presidents, managers and directors, for example -- as there are entry level or midlevel jobs.\par \par \uc2 To find jobs, older workers might reach out to their contacts, stay active in associations within their fields and maintain a social media presence, said Craig Copeland, director, wealth benefits research at \uc2 Employee Benefit Research Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those laid off in their 50s can feel out of step with their peers when Americans are working longer.\par \par \uc2 Since 1991, the average retirement age has increased by about three years, according to Boston College.\par \par \uc2 Reasons include improved health and longevity and the rise of jobs that are less physically demanding. The shift from guaranteed monthly pensions to 401(k) plans made people feel less secure about their money lasting.\par \par \uc2 As retirement ages have risen, so has the average age at which both men and women claim Social Security, to 65 from 63 two decades ago. That trend has been positive for retirement income. For every year between 62 and 70 that recipients delay taking benefits, the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2  increases their monthly benefit checks by up to 8%.\par \par \uc2 Many Americans have a job at some point in their 50s and early 60s without 401(k) and health insurance benefits, according to Boston College's Center for Retirement Research.\par \par \uc2 Some older workers make the switch voluntarily to pursue less remunerative interests or a better work-life balance.\par \par \uc2 Others take such jobs because that is what they can find.\par \par \uc2 The loss of benefits could be a sign of declining job quality for older job switchers, said Chen, the Boston College researcher. Those who frequently work without benefits in their 50s and 60s end up with about 25% less retirement income than workers with such benefits.\par \par \uc2 A widely mentioned strategy for helping improve financial security in retirement is to either work longer at a current job or pick up another job after retiring from a career.\par \par \uc2 One of the ways to extend a career could be taking on a reduced role, such as part-time or project-specific work.\par \par \uc2 This is an appealing choice for many workers, as many want to stay active but don't want to be tied completely to the workplace.\par \par \uc2 However, these types of roles aren't widely available, as they tend to be limited to only those with very specific knowledge or skills, said Copeland, of the \uc2 Employee Benefit Research Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In some cases, people might not think the job search is worth the effort if they only planned on working a couple of more years, he said.\par \par \uc2 Knowing that you might face an involuntary job loss at this age reinforces the benefit of saving as much as you can earlier in your career.\par \par \uc2 "Workers need to understand that the retirement decision will not always be their choice," said Copeland.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250722el7m00011\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
