\par\par \b \uc2 Smaller Cities Lure New Grads\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ray A. Smith and Haley Zimmerman \par \uc2 1,196 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Memo to job-hunting college grads: It pays to take your search to cities just beyond the U.S.'s biggest metro areas.\par \par \uc2 In one of the toughest markets for entry-level jobs in years, several second-tier cities -- including Raleigh, N.C. -- rise above the pack for their strong hiring, decent salaries and affordability. Instead of Atlanta or Chicago, consider Birmingham, Ala., or Milwaukee. Or think Baltimore, in lieu of Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Those alternative cities, along with Austin, Texas, rank as the top five most promising locations to find work for recent college graduates, according to a new study by payroll-services provider ADP.\par \par \uc2 Researchers crunched public cost-of-living data and ADP payroll data for more than five million U.S. workers in their 20s to rank 55 metro areas on three metrics: affordability, wages and hiring activity. They then weighed hiring rates for jobs that typically require a degree against affordability-adjusted wages in each city to devise a single, overall ranking.\par \par \uc2 What sets the top-ranking cities apart? Raleigh, Baltimore and Austin -- all of which scored in last year's top five, too -- tend to have a higher-than-usual concentration of technology, health and financial firms, said Ben Hanowell, ADP Research's director of people analytics.\par \par \uc2 Like Birmingham, they are home to research and healthcare institutions that do a lot of hiring themselves. Milwaukee's job market, meanwhile, is benefiting from the expansion of \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  and other companies in the area.\par \par \uc2 All of those factors are bright spots in an otherwise difficult job market for 20-somethings. Though the national unemployment rate hovers around 4%, it was 7.3% in June for recent college graduates. Many of them are running into a bottleneck in entry-level openings as employers slow hiring and fewer older workers switch jobs. It doesn't help that more companies are relying on artificial intelligence to do some of the grunt work new graduates often do.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot more great people than there are great jobs," says Susan De La Vega, a senior vice president at global consulting firm \uc2 Korn Ferry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those challenges have fueled a lot of fluctuation in local job markets. Atlanta and Charlotte, N.C., both in last year's top five for young grads, dropped considerably in this year's rankings. Portland, Ore., Hartford, Conn., Riverside, Calif., and Salt Lake City all ranked toward the bottom because of slower hiring rates and lower real wages once cost of living was factored in.\par \par \uc2 Raleigh, once again, topped the list of cities with the most promising prospects for recent graduates. As part of the Research Triangle, its economy is fueled by an ecosystem of research institutions, including the \uc2 University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill\uc2 , \uc2 Duke University\uc2  and other schools, plus science and technology companies.\par \par \uc2 "We've seen this stream of office projects over the last 18 months, and \uc2 North Carolina State University\uc2  and some of our private colleges are all supplying this great pipeline of workers," says Kyle Touchstone, director of Raleigh Economic Development.\par \par \uc2 Red Hat\uc2  and \uc2 Cisco Systems\uc2  are just two companies with Raleigh operations that have stepped up their recruiting of young professionals. The Research Triangle area accounted for nearly a third of \uc2 Cisco\uc2 's overall university hiring, second only to Silicon Valley, over the past four fiscal years, said Scott McGuckin, vice president of talent acquisition.\par \par \uc2 Jordin Young, who graduated from \uc2 Bowdoin College\uc2  in Maine in 2024, said he knew just one person who lived in Raleigh before moving there for an associate job at \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 "I knew I wanted to explore a new city," said Young. He was pleasantly surprised by how Raleigh stacked up against New York, Chicago and the other bigger cities his peers were flocking to. It is more affordable and offers an active social scene and easy access to hiking in state parks, he said.\par \par \uc2 Milwaukee, which ranked second, is benefiting from investment from a number of major companies in the area, as well as a burgeoning startup scene. In December, \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  announced a $3 billion manufacturing expansion in nearby Kenosha County that will include 750 highly skilled jobs. \uc2 Northwestern Mutual\uc2  and Milwaukee Tool have recently expanded their footprints in the area as well.\par \par \uc2 The city's affordability compared with Chicago is a draw for both young professionals and companies looking to hire, according to the \uc2 Metropolitan Milwaukee Association of Commerce\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Northwestern Mutual\uc2  has longstanding partnerships with \uc2 Marquette University\uc2  and the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee to tap students for potential jobs. The insurance and wealth-management firm also works with nonprofits to help recruit promising, limited-income college students "we might not otherwise know," said Kelly Culler, the company's chief people officer.\par \par \uc2 Baltimore officials, too, are trying to play up their city's appeal to younger professionals. In March, the Greater Baltimore Committee launched a campaign to promote the metro area's affordability compared with cities like Philadelphia, and its dining scene, parks and museums.\par \par \uc2 Among employers, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  cites the area's higher-education programs and talent market as reasons why the bank's Baltimore office has grown into one of its largest in North America outside New York.\par \par \uc2 Baltimore wasn't on Markeisa Graham's radar before she graduated from \uc2 Florida A&M\uc2  in Tallahassee in 2021. What little she knew about the city came mostly from watching "The Wire" and the show's exploration of Baltimore's criminal drug trade.\par \par \uc2 But after applying to jobs around the country, she landed a job there that fall as a software engineer at \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2 . She was impressed by the friendliness of the people and how walkable the city is. "I was like, 'This is nothing like 'The Wire' at all,'" she said.\par \par \uc2 Austin, on the other hand, has been a destination for college-educated 20-somethings for years. Hiring remains strong because of the expansion of semiconductors, clean tech, AI and other tech industries, according to Austin's chamber of commerce.\par \par \uc2 Though housing costs remain an issue, rents have declined nearly 3% from last year, and the City Council has committed to building more affordable housing, said Eric Hale, director of education and workforce policy at the Austin Chamber.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham is also winning over young professionals. Companies in healthcare, finance and technology are big employers, as is the \uc2 University of Alabama at Birmingham\uc2  and its medical center.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Gill, 22, originally planned to return to Atlanta, his hometown, once he earned his computer science degree from \uc2 Samford University\uc2  in Homewood, Ala. But after graduating in May, he landed a job in Birmingham as a junior quality-assurance analyst at \uc2 Vulcan Materials\uc2 , a supplier of gravel, asphalt and other construction materials.\par \par \uc2 Birmingham's slower pace took some getting used to, he says, but he has come to appreciate living in a smaller city. He has noticed a lot of out-of-state license plates in his company parking lot, which suggests to him that more young professionals are moving there.\par \par \uc2 "More and more I can see myself living in Birmingham" for the long term, he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250719el7j00022\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
