\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Secret Deals, Exploding Offers and Lots of Tears --- The war for AI talent has hit a feverish new peak\b0\par\par\uc2 By Berber Jin, Keach Hagey and Ben Cohen \par \uc2 1,762 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hundreds of employees at one of Silicon Valley's hottest AI startups gathered in their offices last Friday expecting a celebratory announcement.\par \par \uc2 For months, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  had been talking to Windsurf about buying it for $3 billion, and now it seemed like the rank-and-file were finally getting confirmation that the deal was about to become official. Windsurf's marketing team even began filming the all-hands meeting for promotional material.\par \par \uc2 Instead, they learned that Windsurf's chief executive, Varun Mohan, had left the company to join \uc2 Google\uc2 , taking with him a small group of artificial-intelligence researchers and engineers. After hearing the news, some of the staff began to cry.\par \par \uc2 By Monday morning, another twist in the saga would bring those Windsurf employees back to the same room for a second announcement: The rest of their company would be acquired by a rival AI startup.\par \par \uc2 In other words, it was just a typical weekend in Silicon Valley.\par \par \uc2 The war among some of the richest companies on the planet for talent is playing out in an unprecedented frenzy of talent raids, secret deals and betrayals, leading brainy AI researchers whose minds have never been so highly valued to become as rich as \uc2 NBA\uc2  players and Hollywood stars.\par \par \uc2 The most powerful CEOs in tech are dangling pay packages worth more than $300 million to their most prized recruits -- and even that kind of money isn't always enough to win them over.\par \par \uc2 Every feverish new development in the all-out brawl for talent has captivated Silicon Valley. The company at the center of the action is \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which is in the midst of one of the most astonishing recruiting blitzes of all time. Led by \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , who is personally assembling his AI dream team, the company has poached the leaders of promising startups, stunning their investors and employees. It has also given so-called exploding offers that expire within days to potential hires so it's harder for \uc2 Meta\uc2 's rivals to negotiate effective counters.\par \par \uc2 The playbook has left the industry to wonder whether the social, mission-first contract that once united founders and employees is unraveling. Meanwhile, some executives are bemoaning the erosion of what was once a bedrock principle of Silicon Valley: Be a missionary, not a mercenary.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2  described the battle in those terms in a \uc2 Slack\uc2  message he sent to researchers earlier this month when Zuckerberg was attempting to raid his company.\par \par \uc2 "I am proud of how mission-oriented our industry is as a whole," Altman wrote in a message seen by The Wall Street Journal. "Of course there will always be some mercenaries. Missionaries will beat mercenaries."\par \par \uc2 That idea is widely credited to \uc2 John Doerr\uc2 , one of the titanic figures in venture capital. He is now the chairman of the famed VC firm \uc2 Kleiner Perkins\uc2 , which was on the board of Windsurf. For decades, he told generations of entrepreneurs who came to Silicon Valley with dreams of starting a company to embrace their inner missionaries. In mercenary cultures, "the central goal is a lust for making money," Doerr once said. But at missionary companies, "there's a lust not only for making money, but for making meaning."\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  rejects the characterization that its newly hired employees were simply chasing money. Zuckerberg has said that his company's allure isn't the compensation packages but its access to the massive amounts of computing power that researchers need to make breakthroughs.\par \par \uc2 "Meta Superintelligence Labs will have industry-leading levels of compute and by far the greatest compute per researcher," he wrote in a Threads post this past week.\par \par \uc2 But the market for talent has been turned upside down by his willingness to throw colossal sums of money at wonky AI researchers -- a decision partly inspired by a conversation with an influential figure at the company he's targeted the most in his recruitment spree.\par \par \uc2 In the spring, Zuckerberg approached \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's chief research officer, Mark Chen, for a casual catch-up and ended up asking him for advice on how to improve his company's generative-AI organization. Given how much money \uc2 Meta\uc2  was already spending on hardware and computing power to train AI -- more than 100 times what it was spending on humans -- Chen suggested that Zuckerberg might want to invest more in talent, according to people familiar with the conversation.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg asked Chen if he would consider joining \uc2 Meta\uc2  -- and what it would take to bring him aboard.\par \par \uc2 A couple hundred million dollars? A billion?\par \par \uc2 Chen demurred, saying he was happy at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 . But the conversation helped plant the seeds of an idea.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg got to work creating a list of the world's best AI researchers. He fired off emails, texts and WhatsApp messages to potential hires, gauging their interest and inviting some to meet him at his homes in Lake Tahoe and Palo Alto, Calif.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg settled on \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2  to lead the new lab. To get him, \uc2 Meta\uc2  paid $14 billion for a stake in \uc2 Scale AI\uc2 , the data-labeling startup founded by the 28-year-old entrepreneur.\par \par \uc2 On a Friday morning in mid-June, Wang told employees gathered in the plant-filled atrium of Scale's cavernous San Francisco headquarters that he was leaving the company. He walked down the building's staircase to thunderous applause from his staff.\par \par \uc2 For his new AI division focused on superintelligence, or AI that is smarter than humans, Zuckerberg has poached from \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 , \uc2 Google DeepMind\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , and pulled at least a dozen employees from \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But many have turned him down. He's offered more than 10 of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's researchers eye-watering pay packages of $300 million over four years, including $100 million the first year, according to people familiar with the matter. And after months of recruiting, \uc2 Meta\uc2  still doesn't have a chief scientist for its lab.\par \par \uc2 "I've lost track of how many people from here they've tried to get," Altman told his staff in a \uc2 Slack\uc2  message.\par \par \uc2 Still, the pressure has rattled \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 . Chen, whose conversation with Zuckerberg had helped spur \uc2 Meta\uc2 's recruiting push, reassured \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  employees in late June that the company's leadership was responding to the aggressive recruitment by adjusting compensation packages and "scoping out creative ways to recognize and reward our top talent," he wrote in a \uc2 Slack\uc2  message seen by the Journal.\par \par \uc2 He also equated \uc2 Meta\uc2 's talent raid to a burglary: "i feel a visceral feeling right now -- as if someone has broken into our home and stolen something," Chen wrote. "please trust that we haven't been sitting idly by."\par \par \uc2 Among the Meta Superintelligence Lab's buzziest hires are AI investors \uc2 Nat Friedman\uc2  and Daniel Gross.\par \par \uc2 Friedman, the former CEO of the coding platform \uc2 GitHub\uc2 , was hired to be one of the AI lab's leaders alongside Wang.\par \par \uc2 Gross was the CEO of AI company \uc2 Safe Superintelligence\uc2 , or \uc2 SSI\uc2  for short, which he founded last year with former \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  chief scientist Ilya Sutskever, a luminary in the field who left \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  last year after voting with other board members to fire Altman in November 2023.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg received an introduction to Sutskever from Gross. The two had known each other ever since Zuckerberg considered buying Gross's search-engine startup more than a decade ago. In late January, the three of them got together for lunch at Zuckerberg's home in \uc2 Palo Alto\uc2 . Shortly afterward, the \uc2 Meta\uc2  CEO said he was interested in buying \uc2 SSI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sutskever immediately rebuffed the offer and told him that \uc2 SSI\uc2  was not for sale, according to people familiar with the matter. A few weeks later, \uc2 Meta\uc2  invested in an \uc2 SSI\uc2  funding round that valued the startup at $32 billion.\par \par \uc2 Gross told close associates he disagreed with Sutskever's decision not to sell \uc2 SSI\uc2 . In May, he received his own offer to join \uc2 Meta\uc2  alongside Friedman. Sutskever was blindsided when he learned that his CEO and co-founder was defecting to a rival.\par \par \uc2 When the news of Gross's likely move began to leak out in the middle of June, waves of surprise rippled through the text chains of founders, investors and prominent tech CEOs. They were stunned that he would abandon his startup so quickly for a competitor.\par \par \uc2 "I have incredible empathy for Daniel. This was an unimaginably hard decision," said Shaun Maguire, a \uc2 Sequoia\uc2  partner who helped lead the firm's investment in \uc2 SSI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many of the most lucrative offers have been extended to researchers who were already working for leading AI labs, but the latest battlegrounds in the talent wars are the industry's most promising startups -- like Windsurf.\par \par \uc2 The most recent drama in Silicon Valley began months ago, when \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  agreed to buy Windsurf for $3 billion. But the deal process dragged on and eventually broke down when \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's largest investor, objected to some of the terms. After the exclusivity window for negotiations with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  expired, Windsurf turned to a backup plan.\par \par \uc2 Mohan, the Windsurf CEO, quickly struck a $2.4 billion deal with \uc2 Google\uc2 . \uc2 Google\uc2  got access to the startup's technology and hired away some of its key employees.\par \par \uc2 These so-called acquihire deals have become a common strategy for Silicon Valley giants to evade antitrust scrutiny, but they leave the remaining employees working for companies that are shells of their former selves. In the case of Windsurf, hundreds of recent hires were left empty-handed and feeling abandoned by their leaders. When they walked out of the meeting last Friday afternoon, their futures were as uncertain as the company's.\par \par \uc2 "I want to acknowledge that this is extremely shocking news and it will take at least 24 hours to digest it," Windsurf's new CEO, Jeff Wang, said during the emotional all-hands. "It might take all weekend."\par \par \uc2 But before the weekend began, Wang received an email at 5:30 p.m. from Scott Wu, the CEO of coding startup Cognition, with the subject line: "chat?"\par \par \uc2 Wu and Wang chatted and struck a deal on Saturday afternoon for Cognition to buy the rest of Windsurf.\par \par \uc2 The two spent the rest of the weekend hunkered down with their colleagues in Windsurf's offices hammering out the deal. Wang told employees on Monday morning that all of them would get paid out as part of the deal, regardless of whether their equity had vested.\par \par \uc2 This time, he received a standing ovation.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250719el7j0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
