\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: New Jobless Claims Fell To 221,000 Last Week\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matt Grossman \par \uc2 210 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The number of Americans who newly filed for unemployment benefits declined last week, the Labor Department said Thursday, another reassuring sign about the health of the job market following a spike in claims this spring.\par \par \uc2 In the week through July 12, new jobless-claim filings fell to 221,000, from a revised 228,000 a week earlier. Economists polled by The Wall Street Journal were forecasting 234,000 claims.\par \par \uc2 Continuing claims, an indicator of the size of the total unemployed population, came in at 1.96 million in the week through July 5, compared with 1.95 million a week earlier. The continuing-claim data lag the initial-claim data by a week and have been trending higher, in a sign that it has become harder for unemployed people to find work.\par \par \uc2 This spring, three straight weeks brought reports of more than 245,000 initial claims, a run that looked to some analysts like the start of more serious deterioration in the jobs market. But the tally has subsided again in the past three weeks.\par \par \uc2 While monthly job creation has slowed, the unemployment rate has held close to steady in recent months at 4.2%.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich401\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6015
