\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Your Prize for Saving Time At Work With AI: More Work --- Tech allows workers to accomplish more, in less time, but that creates a new conflict on the job\b0\par\par\uc2 By Callum Borchers \par \uc2 953 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 We're past the point of imagining how artificial intelligence could help us work more efficiently. The question now is whether we can keep the hours we save, or whether the reward for working faster is. . .more work.\par \par \uc2 Breezing through the tedious items on our to-do lists is supposed to make us see AI as an ally, instead of a threat. But unless finishing early comes with extra downtime or other gains, the real beneficiaries are likely to be businesses looking to squeeze more productivity out of their teams.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't sound, for example, as though lighter workloads are in store for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees. Chief Executive \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2  recently urged them to "figure out how to invent for our customers more quickly and expansively, and how to get more done with scrappier teams" using AI.\par \par \uc2 The clear message from him and other business leaders is that we can't simply do as much work as we've been doing, in less time, and clock out early. If we do, we risk being replaced by someone who uses AI to increase output.\par \par \uc2 Still, workers aren't just going to accept this without a fight.\par \par \uc2 A recent survey found nearly half of workers believe their AI time savings should belong to them, not their employers. That survey, conducted by business-software maker SAP, also found that workers using AI save almost an hour a day on average.\par \par \uc2 More than one-fifth of the survey respondents said they would rather hide that hour than give managers a reason to expect more. \uc2 Cue\uc2  the \uc2 Zillow\uc2  browsing while pretending to work and other forms of "productivity theater."\par \par \uc2 How can companies keep this from turning into a new workplace battleground?\par \par \uc2 They need to minimize secrecy and resentment to get the most out of emerging technology, says Autumn Krauss, chief scientist on SAP's market insights and customer engagement team. That means making employees feel as though they receive something more enticing than a heavier workload.\par \par \uc2 "We have customers in legal and professional services who have taken a more progressive, strategic approach," she says. "They're saying, 'We're not just going to require more billable hours. We're going to allow you to reallocate that time to professional development.'"\par \par \uc2 That way, employees can use the time AI frees up to position themselves for future promotions and raises.\par \par \uc2 Of course, not every company will use that approach. Or employees will just take things into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 Consider Jeff Mette. While some people shave minutes by using ChatGPT to draft emails, he is an AI ninja who saves substantially more time by employing multiple tools, each for a specific purpose.\par \par \uc2 Mette, a managing director at a software consulting firm in Atlanta, leans on Gemini for research. He relies on \uc2 Perplexity\uc2  to summarize the news. He trained Claude to mimic his writing style.\par \par \uc2 Altogether, he estimates what used to be 60 hours of work can now be completed in 30. This is largely because AI gets him up to speed on prospective clients and their competitors much faster than his old process of poring over financial documents and media reports manually.\par \par \uc2 "I started a side hustle because of things I found I could do with AI," says Mette, 52. Since January, he has independently advised small businesses on go-to-market strategies.\par \par \uc2 He feels he owes his primary employer 40 hours every week. But he also thinks it's reasonable to reclaim time beyond the full-time standard and put it into something of his own.\par \par \uc2 At his primary job, he now budgets an hour for coffee meetings he once crammed into 30 minutes. The slower pace produces more creative sparks, he says.\par \par \uc2 It's one thing to transfer time saved to a second job or professional-development course. It's another to actually work less.\par \par \uc2 History is not on employees' side in this regard.\par \par \uc2 "New technologies that speed up aspects of knowledge work tend to just lead to faster-paced knowledge work," says Cal Newport, the "Slow Productivity" author who advocates working at a natural pace to do fewer tasks well. "The advent of PCs and email to the office, for example, led only to explosions in the average employee's workload."\par \par \uc2 This is partly because output is hard to measure in many jobs, so companies often use time as a proxy.\par \par \uc2 The best bet for people hoping to truly cut back might be for businesses to realize employees have limited reserves of deep concentration. AI is supposed to free us of mundane tasks so we can focus on the important things, but can we really think big thoughts eight hours a day, five days a week?\par \par \uc2 "It's quite possible that by taking the low-demand stuff off our plates we're piling up too much high-demand stuff," says economist and sociologist Juliet Schor, whose new book, "Four Days a Week," chronicles companies' experiments with reduced schedules.\par \par \uc2 She worries U.S. workers' already-high rates of stress and burnout could get worse if they are expected to work long hours at AI-aided levels of efficiency. Most of the businesses she studied that tried four-day workweeks found their employees could accomplish as much as they used to, or more, if given an extra day to recharge.\par \par \uc2 But pared-down schedules remain far from mainstream. For that to change, workers might first have to be pushed to their breaking points. Companies will respond if -- perhaps only if -- their AI productivity drives backfire.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250714el7e0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
