\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- \uc2 Google\uc2  Strikes Deal With AI Coding Startup Windsurf\b0\par\par\uc2 By Berber Jin and Katherine Blunt \par \uc2 356 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  has agreed to pay about $2.4 billion in a deal to license the technology of AI coding startup Windsurf and also hire its CEO and some of its employees, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The deal comes after acquisition talks for \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  to acquire Windsurf stalled, the people said.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  is hiring a small number of Windsurf's employees to focus on agentic coding within its DeepMind division and the tech giant will also acquire a nonexclusive license to some of Windsurf's technology.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  isn't taking a stake in Windsurf. Most of Windsurf's existing employees will remain at the company.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  first struck an agreement to buy the startup for $3 billion in April in an effort to boost the growth of its AI coding products.\par \par \uc2 But the deal hit a roadblock after \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's largest investor, objected to some of the terms, The Wall Street Journal reported.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  didn't want to give \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  access to Windsurf's technology. Under their current contract, the tech giant is entitled to all of the startup's intellectual property.\par \par \uc2 The failed talks are a setback for \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , which is racing against other tech companies to build AI coding assistants, one of the largest new markets enabled by generative AI, and shows how tensions with its largest partner are hampering major business efforts.\par \par \uc2 It is also another example of the pitched battle going on between Silicon Valley giants to hire and retain the most talented AI scientists and engineers.\par \par \uc2 Tech giants have pursued similar "acquihire" deals as a way to attract top startup talent while avoiding the scrutiny of antitrust regulators. \uc2 Google\uc2  last year paid $2.7 billion to hire its former researcher Noam Shazeer and license technology from the startup he started, \uc2 Character.AI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  have also struck similar deals with startups.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  negotiated its deal with Windsurf after the exclusivity window for talks between \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  fell through. Tech news site The Verge earlier reported on some aspects of \uc2 Google\uc2 's deal.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250712el7c0002d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
