\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 The President Can Fire People\b0\par\par\uc2 347 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Do you hear the Justices now? That's the question for lower court judges after the Supreme Court on Tuesday nixed a universal injunction that had barred the Trump Administration from even attempting to shrink the federal workforce.\par \par \uc2 Federal worker unions challenged a February executive order (Trump v. \uc2 AFGE\uc2 ) directing agency heads to "promptly undertake preparations to initiate large-scale reductions in force (RIFs), consistent with applicable law." A lower court in May blocked the \uc2 EO\uc2 , barring the President's appointees from making plans on how to implement large-scale layoffs.\par \par \uc2 The Justices stayed the injunction in a pithy order, noting "the Government is likely to succeed on its argument that the Executive Order and Memorandum are lawful." They also stressed that "we express no view on the legality of any Agency RIF and Reorganization Plan produced or approved pursuant to the Executive Order and Memorandum."\par \par \uc2 Only Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  dissented. The order includes "no mention of congressional buy-in," she writes. But Congress does not control the executive branch. "What is at issue here," Justice Jackson says, is whether the order effects "a massive restructuring of the Federal Government (the likes of which have historically required Congress's approval), on the one hand, or minor workforce reductions consistent with existing law, on the other. One needs facts to answer that critical question." Precisely.\par \par \uc2 That's why it was premature for the lower court to enjoin the \uc2 EO\uc2 . As Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  stresses in a concurrence, the agency reorganization "plans themselves are not before this Court, at this stage, and we thus have no occasion to consider whether they can and will be carried out consistent with the constraints of law."\par \par \uc2 It's also not clear at this stage whether the plaintiffs will suffer a concrete injury. Federal Judge Susan Illston sought to tie the hands of agency heads on the presumption that their reorganizations might break the law. Justices are sending a useful message to lower courts not to reach legal conclusions before they even see what agencies are proposing.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250709el7900012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
