\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Late-Career Job Loss Blurs Retirement --- Four people open up about finances and how they spend their time after a sudden change\b0\par\par\uc2 By Veronica Dagher and Anne Tergesen \par \uc2 1,757 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One man who lost his job in his early 60s is transitioning into retirement by working 25 hours a week. A woman who recently got laid off in her 50s worries about her finances if she doesn't get a new job.\par \par \uc2 A bumpy transition often awaits people who get laid off or take buyouts late in their careers. For many, the line between working years and retirement starts to blur. It is a growing concern for people with jobs in the federal government or at private companies cutting jobs.\par \par \uc2 Economic uncertainty has persisted in recent months, though employers continued to add jobs in June.\par \par \uc2 For older people who want to stay in the workforce, a layoff can be followed by a longer spell of unemployment. It takes nearly 26 weeks, on average, for people ages 55 to 64 to find a job, compared with 19 weeks for people ages 25 to 34, according to the \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 People laid off at higher ages are less likely to move into a new field than those who quit, said Kevin Cahill, an economist at \uc2 FTI Consulting\uc2 . Ageism and higher compensation expectations can be obstacles to re-employment, he said. Older career workers who find new jobs take an 11% pay cut, on average, according to a forthcoming study Cahill co-wrote.\par \par \uc2 Those who retire might need to break into their savings early. They might miss out on making 401(k) or IRA contributions, never mind the catch-up contributions allowed for those 50 and older.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with four people who had late-career layoffs or buyouts.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Crain\par \par \uc2 Gloucester, Mass., and New York City\par \par \uc2 Current age: 64\par \par \uc2 Annual spending: $240,000\par \par \uc2 Kevin Crain\uc2  was the head of retirement research at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 . Then, in 2023, the bank offered him early retirement.\par \par \uc2 He never imagined the life he studied could be so satisfying.\par \par \uc2 Crain assumed he would work full-bore until age 65. After getting past disappointment at retiring two years sooner than expected, he "quickly pivoted into a retirement philosophy of, what's next?"\par \par \uc2 Crain, 64, has built a second act that includes part-time paid work as a consultant and the director of a retirement industry association focused on 401(k) products and services that produce a retirement income. He provides free advice to two nonprofits on aging and two startups he has invested in.\par \par \uc2 He makes less money but enjoys having control over his time. He typically works about five hours a day, leaving time for the gym and reading.\par \par \uc2 He used to assume he would retire to a life of leisure. But his father's retirement experience dissuaded him. "Dad went from 100 mph to zero and never did anything else," said Crain, who believes this left his father vulnerable to dementia.\par \par \uc2 Crain said the transition was easy because he was already working from home. He and his wife Rebecca Crain, 63, split their time between New York City and Gloucester, Mass., where they recently purchased a home.\par \par \uc2 Crain\uc2  earns about $110,000 a year, which supplements distributions from cash and executive deferred compensation and restricted stock programs. Together, they replace about 80% of his preretirement pay.\par \par \uc2 In 2027, the restricted stock and deferred compensation will run out and the couple will begin tapping their retirement and brokerage accounts, which have 90% in fixed income. They plan to delay claiming Social Security until age 70.\par \par \uc2 Their net worth is more than $5 million and includes retirement accounts with $1.9 million. Their only debt is a $360,000 mortgage, with a rate of 2.8%.\par \par \uc2 The couple spend about $23,000 a month, half on their mortgage and rent for their Manhattan apartment. Premiums for health insurance run about $2,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 Given his low tolerance for investment risk, Crain is happy to be relatively shielded from the stock market's volatility. "Having had heart issues, I don't want the financial stress," he said.\par \par \uc2 Drew Myers\par \par \uc2 The Villages, Fla.\par \par \uc2 Current age: 68\par \par \uc2 Annual spending: $96,000\par \par \uc2 Drew Myers planned to work until he was 70. But the technology salesman wasn't devastated to be laid off at 67.\par \par \uc2 Getting about $70,000 in severance helped.\par \par \uc2 He initially looked for another job in tech. He got first-round interviews, but he hit subsequent roadblocks that he attributed to ageism.\par \par \uc2 Since he was no longer working, he had the time to care for his father, who was dying of cancer. For about the first year of his retirement, he shuttled his dad to doctor's appointments, made sure his dad ate and kept him company before he died.\par \par \uc2 Retirement didn't feel real for Drew and his wife, Anne Marie O'Connell-Myers, until April, when they moved from Connecticut to the Villages, Fla., a large retirement community.\par \par \uc2 It took time to adjust to withdrawing money from their retirement accounts and no longer having paychecks. Their expenses have been higher than they would have liked for most of this year, largely because of the move. Now that they are settling in, they expect their monthly spending to drop to about $8,000, from roughly $10,000. That includes about $1,500 toward health insurance premiums.\par \par \uc2 The couple has about $1.3 million in retirement savings. He has a pension of about $800 a month from a former employer and about $4,090 a month in Social Security. They have about $16,000 in timeshare debt.\par \par \uc2 They are renting a three-bedroom home for a year before they commit to buying in the Villages. They anticipate spending about $350,000, which they plan to pay for with funds he will inherit from the sale of his father's house.\par \par \uc2 They are enjoying daily swims and golfing several times a week, with plans to take up pickleball.\par \par \uc2 Brenda Pitts\par \par \uc2 Brooklyn, N.Y.\par \par \uc2 Current age: 56\par \par \uc2 Annual spending: $156,000\par \par \uc2 Brenda Pitts got laid off in March. She doesn't consider herself retired, but if she can't find a new job, she's concerned she will have to rethink her plan to retire in New York.\par \par \uc2 The Brooklyn resident, 56, is networking and enjoying having more free time in the city.\par \par \uc2 She is looking to keep working in the financial-services industry, but has found many companies currently have hiring freezes.\par \par \uc2 She misses her colleagues and is glad many have reached out.\par \par \uc2 Pitts has always feared falling back into the financial insecurity she grew up with. She is the youngest of five children who were raised by a custodian and a roofer. "I was always worried about being one check away from disaster," said Pitts, who attended \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 , largely on scholarship, and got an MBA.\par \par \uc2 While in her 30s, Pitts paid off her student loans and started saving for retirement.\par \par \uc2 In her 40s, she left Silicon Valley and moved to New York to work in financial services, where she saw more women working in their 50s.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, she has been saving the annual maximum allowed in both her IRA and 401(k). Last year, she put about $80,000 into the two, including her employer's match and the catch-up contributions people 50 and older can make.\par \par \uc2 She currently has $2.3 million saved. More recently, she put her contributions on hold. She worries her $4 million goal might be unattainable.\par \par \uc2 Pitts works about four hours a day on her job search and consults free to nonprofits. She is a member of museums and film societies, and she enjoys exhibits, movies and live music -- everything from punk to opera.\par \par \uc2 She spends about $13,000 a month, including $5,175 on her mortgage and maintenance fees, $1,400 on health-related costs and $3,000 for food and entertainment.\par \par \uc2 Pitts has stopped most of her charitable contributions for now, but she hopes to use some of her savings to help young people from financially disadvantaged families.\par \par \uc2 "I don't have children of my own, but I'd like my legacy to be that I made the path easier for younger people who come behind me," she said.\par \par \uc2 Cartier Snyder\par \par \uc2 Plano, Texas\par \par \uc2 Current age: 70\par \par \uc2 Annual spending: $108,000\par \par \uc2 For years, Cartier Snyder, a veteran of the printing business, watched the industry change.\par \par \uc2 By his early 60s, most clients had shifted to digital. Demand for printed materials such as corporate annual reports had plummeted.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, Snyder accepted an offer to retire from his longtime employer in return for healthcare coverage until he qualified for Medicare the following year. Initially, Snyder, who had planned to retire at 67, worried about whether he and his wife, Barbara Snyder, would have enough money.\par \par \uc2 A health scare about six years before the buyout helped him put retirement in perspective. An X-ray for a persistent cough revealed a mass in the former marathon runner's chest. This turned out to be an aneurysm and led to the discovery of a failing heart valve. After three surgeries, he was expected to live about five to 10 years.\par \par \uc2 He decided retiring earlier than planned was a good thing. "I didn't want to die at my desk at work," Snyder said.\par \par \uc2 Their financial adviser assured them they would be fine.\par \par \uc2 The couple have about $500,000 in retirement accounts and receive about $6,000 a month in Social Security. They also get an additional $1,900 a month in rental income from a duplex they own and live in.\par \par \uc2 The Plano, Texas, couple spend around $9,000 a month. They have about $100,000 left on the duplex's mortgage.\par \par \uc2 It took him several months to adapt to the slower pace of retirement after decades of going to the office. Now 70, he also had to get used to not being the boss. Barbara, who had retired as a nurse at 59, was accustomed to having the house to herself during the day.\par \par \uc2 Having more time to spend with their grandchildren helped, they both said. He heads to the gym three mornings a week around 5:30 a.m. to lift weights with his son. Barbara enjoys quilting for charity. They garden together.\par \par \uc2 "You need to find your new purpose once you leave your job," he said.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: A Hidden Aspect of Job Loss" -- WSJ July 16, 2025)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250708el780000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
