\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Brandy Is Caught in Trade War As Chinese, U.S. Tariffs Bite --- China said it would impose duties while separate U.S. dispute is at critical juncture\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Kostov and Kim Mackrael \par \uc2 711 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 If the global trade wars had an official drink, it would be fine French brandy.\par \par \uc2 The preferred quaff of aristocrats and rappers is a key export for European luxury-drink makers, which are fighting tariff assaults from its two biggest markets: China and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, China said it would impose duties of up to 34.9% on European brandy imports, following an investigation that concluded producers were dumping the spirit on the market, according to a statement from the Ministry of Commerce. The move is widely seen by European officials as a response to EU tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The challenges to brandy and its most lucrative variety, cognac, illustrate Europe's distress in defending its industries against the world's economic superpowers.\par \par \uc2 There was a silver lining for major French brands including Hennessy, Martell and Remy Martin: They struck agreements with China to sidestep the tariffs by agreeing to set minimum export prices. Nevertheless, the move leaves the drink makers constrained in their biggest market.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a separate trade dispute with the U.S. is nearing a critical juncture. A 90-day suspension of some American tariffs on EU goods, including French spirits, is due to expire on July 9. Drink makers worry that without an agreement, the EU may impose tariffs on American liquor, which would reanimate a previous threat from President Trump to impose 200% tariffs on European alcohol.\par \par \uc2 The industry had been hit by "two crises in its two biggest markets," leaving producers "almost down on both knees," said Thibault Delrieu, managing director of Hine Cognac.\par \par \uc2 Cognac is a type of brandy produced under strict appellation rules in the west of France. To carry the name, it must be distilled twice from specific white grape varieties and aged in French oak barrels for a minimum of two years, all within the tightly defined Cognac region.\par \par \uc2 Cognac's success in recent decades has been driven as much by cultural cachet as the liquid itself. In the U.S., it gained a devoted following in the 1990s and 2000s thanks to its embrace by hip-hop artists who celebrated the drink as a symbol of luxury and success. In China, cognac flourished among the country's growing class of wealthy consumers, who were drawn to its exclusivity, French heritage and association with refinement. High-end cognac was frequently gifted, poured at banquets or consumed in luxury nightclubs.\par \par \uc2 About 97% of cognac production is sold outside France, with the U.S. and China as its two largest markets. But sales to China collapsed after Beijing launched an antidumping probe into European brandy and began imposing provisional tariffs. Industry groups and executives, including \uc2 LVMH\uc2 's \uc2 Bernard Arnault\uc2 , have warned that thousands of jobs are at risk across the region.\par \par \uc2 The minimum pricing arrangement with China mirrors a similar mechanism used in past trade disputes, allowing exporters to avoid punitive tariffs by committing to sell at or above a set price. The Bureau National Interprofessionnel du Cognac, which represents the industry, said the agreement doesn't imply any admission of dumping and described the measure as "less unfavorable than the anti-dumping tariffs."\par \par \uc2 Still, the industry body said producers remain at a disadvantage, with conditions worse than they were before China's antidumping investigation began in January 2024.\par \par \uc2 While smaller producers have laid off workers or put staff on partial unemployment programs, even France's biggest cognac houses have been feeling the strain.\par \par \uc2 Moet Hennessy\uc2 , the \uc2 LVMH\uc2 -owned maker of Hennessy, reported a 17% drop in cognac and spirits sales in the first quarter of the year, and said it would cut around 1,200 jobs, roughly 12% of its staff. Other major producers, including Martell, Remy Martin and Courvoisier, also have suffered steep sales declines in China.\par \par \uc2 The pressure has intensified tensions at home. Last year, Hennessy provoked an outcry in France's Charente region after unions said it was considering bottling some cognac in China to bypass tariffs. Hundreds of employees at its main factory in Cognac walked out in protest.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250707el770001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
