\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 A Trashy Fourth of July in Philadelphia\b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Public unions are often at odds with the public interest, and Exhibit A is Philadelphia this week. Over the Fourth of July weekend, the home of Independence Hall is greeting holiday guests with garbage piles on streets and limited city services. That's thanks to one of the city's biggest worker strikes in decades.\par \par \uc2 The city's District Council 33, an affiliate of the \uc2 American Federation of State, County and Municipal Employees\uc2  representing some 9,000 workers, went on strike just after 12 a.m. Tuesday. Initially demanding an 8% pay raise for each of the next three years, along with benefit increases, the union had backed down Monday night to 5%, the Philadelphia Inquirer reports. But Mayor Cherelle Parker's offer of a 2.75% raise, followed by two years of 3% hikes and on top of a 5% raise in the last contract, wasn't enough for the union.\par \par \uc2 The union chose to strike as soon as its previous contract expired, and the decision, well, is starting to stink. Members include garbage collectors, and trash is piling up as residents haul their refuse to select locations. "Dumpsters that the city has set up as trash drop-off locations were overflowing with refuse Wednesday, with a notable stench infusing the muggy air," the Inquirer reports. "This is a nightmare," resident Renee Dennis told an ABC affiliate.\par \par \uc2 Union members also include 911 dispatchers and water plant workers. A judge ordered these employees back to their posts Tuesday, but a city website warns 911 callers that they "may still experience longer wait times." The city solicitor said the medical examiner's office was "critically understaffed," and a judge also ordered those workers back Thursday. Better wait to have that heart attack.\par \par \uc2 Several city pools and libraries are also closed, and recreation centers have reduced hours. Some union members interrupted traffic while protesting Thursday. The city also had to scramble to find help for its Fourth of July festivities, though rapper LL Cool J refused to cross a picket line to perform.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Parker said her current offer will cost $115 million over three years, the Inquirer reports. Meanwhile, the Philadelphia teachers union voted last month to authorize a strike if negotiations fall apart when its contract expires in August. Taxpayers will be paying for this city disservice, a reminder of how public unions can hold a city hostage.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250705el750001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
