\par\par \b \uc2 Companies Cut Back On Plans to Add Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Konrad Putzier and Lauren Weber \par \uc2 1,106 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. job growth looked solid in June, but the headline number hides a stark reality: Many private employers are no longer hiring.\par \par \uc2 Weighed down by high interest rates, a federal crackdown on immigration and uncertainty over tariffs, more companies are deciding they are better off with a smaller head count.\par \par \uc2 Last month, businesses added 74,000 new jobs, an anemic number compared with previous months. Private-sector job growth fell to the lowest level since October 2024. Of the 147,000 total new jobs added in June, nearly half were in government, as state and local hiring jumped.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, more than half of private industries cut jobs in June, Labor Department data indicate -- only the third time this has happened since April 2020. For most of the postpandemic period, most industries were adding jobs.\par \par \uc2 Many employers said there are just too many question marks hanging over the economy to expand head count right now. \uc2 Warby Parker\uc2  this spring told investors it would slow the pace of its hiring and reduce other expenses to help profitability.\par \par \uc2 American Vinegar Works, a six-year-old specialty-food company based in Worcester, Mass., is holding off plans to bring on an additional employee to fill orders, as well as sales staff, because of concerns about the economy.\par \par \uc2 "I just don't know what's happening," said the founder, Rodrigo Vargas. "It makes me cautious."\par \par \uc2 The company, which has two full-time and three part-time employees, is delaying planned investments in new equipment for a product launch. "Anything that is longer-term, that would provide results in five or six months, we are being cautious about it," he said.\par \par \uc2 While private employers still added jobs overall in June, the gains were smaller than in prior months and heavily concentrated in a handful of sectors. Healthcare and social assistance, leisure and hospitality and construction companies added 94,000 jobs in June, but they account for just 36% of all private, nonfarm employment. The remaining sectors, which include professional and business services, manufacturing and wholesale trade, cut 20,000 jobs in aggregate.\par \par \uc2 That heavy reliance on a handful of industries increases the risk that overall job growth will fall further in the coming months, said \uc2 David Seif\uc2 , chief economist for developed markets at \uc2 Nomura\uc2 . "It's certainly a red flag," he said.\par \par \uc2 Economists don't see a quick turnaround. A weak labor market "will be a persistent issue in the second half of the year as uncertainty holds back firm hiring," said \uc2 Dante DeAntonio\uc2 , senior director of economic research at \uc2 Moody's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Companies across the economy have plans to trim head count. In June, \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  announced it would cut 7,000 jobs -- or 15% of its nonmanufacturing workforce -- to create "broader roles and smaller teams." \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  said this past week it plans to cut 9,000 workers, after eliminating 6,000 across its product- and software-development teams in May.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  said it planned to offer buyouts to its delivery drivers for the first time in its 117-year history. \uc2 UPS\uc2  is navigating "an unprecedented business landscape" and reorganizing its network, a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 One factor behind the drop in hiring is the federal crackdown on immigration, economists said. Fewer immigrants mean fewer people looking for jobs, which makes it harder for companies to hire, particularly in such sectors as hotels, restaurants and building services.\par \par \uc2 "Industries that are more reliant on immigrants have seen slower job growth in recent months," said \uc2 Jed Kolko\uc2 , a nonresident senior fellow at the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 . At the same time, the drop in immigration means fewer new jobs are needed to keep the unemployment rate steady.\par \par \uc2 Tariff uncertainty is weighing on manufacturers, which often get components and materials from abroad. Manufacturing employment fell in June for the second straight month, data from the Labor Department shows. The drop was particularly noticeable, Kolko said, in high-wage manufacturing sectors such as semiconductors and machinery, which did well in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Some employers are curbing hiring in anticipation that artificial intelligence can do the work. \uc2 Shopify\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Tobi Lutke\uc2 , said in a staff memo this spring that the Canada-based e-commerce company wouldn't make new hires unless managers could prove AI wasn't capable of doing the job.\par \par \uc2 Longer-term, more company bosses are warning that the technology could shrink corporate workforces altogether. "Artificial intelligence is going to replace literally half of all white-collar workers in the U.S.," \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  said in a recent interview with the author \uc2 Walter Isaacson\uc2  at the Aspen Ideas Festival.\par \par \uc2 "At large, we're not really looking to grow our head count very dramatically," \uc2 Adobe\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Shantanu Narayen\uc2  told investors last month. "We are finding a lot more efficiency. People are using AI to be more efficient within the enterprise."\par \par \uc2 Businesses that are hiring are applying a high level of circumspection. At \uc2 Splunk\uc2 , an IT security company acquired by \uc2 Cisco Systems\uc2  last year, every new job has to be approved at the C-suite level, said Dustin Cann, a senior director of talent acquisition strategy and operations there.\par \par \uc2 "If I know I need to get my chief people officer's or CEO's approval, it makes you think, Do I really need it?" he said.\par \par \uc2 The slowdown is hitting young people and recent graduates particularly hard, but older job seekers feel it too.\par \par \uc2 Erin Gramley, who was a marketing executive at a Fortune 500 company before taking time out of the workforce to raise her children, has been looking for work for a few years. Lately, she said, "I'm definitely seeing fewer openings, and the openings you do notice are repeats."\par \par \uc2 Gramley, who is 48 and lives in the Philadelphia area, has been expanding her search outside insurance, her prior industry. She has applied for marketing and project-management jobs in healthcare, education and other sectors. "I'm thinking outside the box about where I can apply my background and skills," she said. "But I'm really not having any luck besides short-term gigs like substitute teaching, anything where they just need bodies."\par \par \uc2 Employers say they are seeing more applicants for every job opening, and they sense a new level of anxiety among job seekers.\par \par \uc2 "There's just a fear and desperation in the candidate population that I haven't seen in a very, very long time," said Tom Lott, the head of talent acquisition at Berry Appleman & Leiden, a law firm in the Dallas area.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw603\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9045\pichgoal7200
