\par\par \b \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  to Lay Off 9,000 More --- Latest round comes on top of the 6,000 roles the company eliminated in May\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kelly Cloonan \par \uc2 351 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  plans to cut an additional 9,000 workers in its latest round of layoffs, bringing its workforce reductions to 15,000 in the past two months.\par \par \uc2 The cuts to staff represent less than 4% of the tech giant's global workforce and span levels, geographies, tenure and teams, a company spokesman said. Job cuts hit \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's gaming segment but didn't account for most of the staff reductions.\par \par \uc2 The latest round of layoffs adds to about 6,000 roles that \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  eliminated in May across its product and software development teams.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal previously reported the company planned to cut thousands more jobs across its sales department and other teams at the start of its new fiscal year, which began Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "We continue to implement organizational and workforce changes that are necessary to position the company and teams for success in a dynamic marketplace," a \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  spokesperson said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Companies across sectors, from retailers to pharmaceutical firms, have been planning to consolidate positions, as they look to boost productivity with leaner staffs. Many say they will rely on technology to perform additional tasks. Other tech companies, including \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 , have said they plan to reduce their workforces in the coming years because increasing use of artificial intelligence will eliminate the need for certain roles.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  sells AI tools to other companies that are meant to automate some tasks and has been investing heavily in data centers. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  had about 228,000 employees at the end of the fiscal year that ended in June 2024. The company eliminated 10,000 jobs in January 2023, retrenching after a pandemic-driven hiring frenzy by many tech companies. The company has already made some cuts to its videogame division last year after closing its acquisition of \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We continue to focus on building high-performing teams and increasing our agility by reducing layers," \uc2 Amy Hood\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's chief financial officer, told investors and analysts in April.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich468\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7020
