\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- It's Known As The List, And It's a Secret File Of Geniuses --- Only select AI researchers have the skills for the hottest area in tech. \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  and his rivals want to hire them -- even if it costs ungodly sums.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Cohen, Berber Jin and Meghan Bobrowsky \par \uc2 1,871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 All over Silicon Valley, the brightest minds in AI are buzzing about "The List," a compilation of the most talented engineers and researchers in artificial intelligence that \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  has spent months putting together.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Beyer works in multimodal vision-language research and describes himself as "a scientist dedicated to the creation of awesomeness." Yu Zhang specializes in automatic speech recognition and barely has an online presence besides his influential papers. Misha Bilenko is an expert in large-scale machine learning who also enjoys hiking and skiing -- or, as he puts it on his website, "applying hill-climbing search and gradient descent algorithms to real-world domains."\par \par \uc2 The recruits on "The List" typically have Ph.D.s from elite schools like Berkeley and Carnegie Mellon. They have experience at places like \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  in San Francisco and \uc2 Google DeepMind\uc2  in London. They are usually in their 20s and 30s -- and they all know each other. They spend their days staring at screens to solve the kinds of inscrutable problems that require spectacular amounts of computing power.\par \par \uc2 And their previously obscure talents have never been so highly valued.\par \par \uc2 The chief executives of tech goliaths and heavyweight venture capitalists are cozying up with a few dozen nerdy researchers because their specialized knowledge is the key to cashing in on the artificial-intelligence revolution.\par \par \uc2 Nobody in this rapidly escalating arms race is chasing the prized recruits quite like Zuckerberg, who has tried to raid Silicon Valley's top research labs, dangling $100 million pay packages to a select few superstars with the hopes of poaching them.\par \par \uc2 The CEO of \uc2 Meta\uc2  wants them to join his company's new lab focused on superintelligence, or AI that is smarter than humans, after the release of its latest model in April fell flat.\par \par \uc2 One recruit who has spoken with Zuckerberg, who is personally courting his dream team of potential hires, describes \uc2 Meta\uc2 's goal as nothing less than a "transfusion from the country's top AI labs."\par \par \uc2 The universe of engineers with deep experience pursuing this type of AI research is tiny. Their loyalty to each other transcends companies. As they decide whether to leave for \uc2 Meta\uc2 , they are comparing notes, trading intel and plotting their futures together. They're trying to figure out who else is joining the lab -- and who else is on "The List." Some are teaming up as package deals. Others are negotiating lavish counteroffers to stay at their companies.\par \par \uc2 So who are these people -- and why have some of the world's richest companies decided they are worth so much?\par \par \uc2 In the secretive world of building ever more powerful AI models, labs have begun to take drastic steps to protect their scientific breakthroughs.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  and \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , researchers work on separate, access-restricted floors, where the blinds are often drawn to guard against prying eyes. At \uc2 Safe Superintelligence\uc2 , the few candidates who secure in-person interviews are asked to leave their phone in a Faraday cage, a container that blocks cellular and Wi-Fi signals. \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 's leaders became so worried that their researchers would be targets of foreign espionage that they once invited an agent from the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  to visit headquarters and describe the risks to employees.\par \par \uc2 The leader of Zuckerberg's handpicked Superintelligence team is \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2 , 28, who grew up in New Mexico as the son of Chinese immigrant physicists at \uc2 Los Alamos National Laboratory\uc2 . He began making plans to create a company in the ninth grade, when he and a friend created a \uc2 Google\uc2  Doc of startup ideas. This month, \uc2 Meta\uc2  shelled out $14 billion for a stake in his company, \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  -- and Zuckerberg made Wang one of the priciest hires of all time.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  has made offers to dozens of researchers at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 . The startup has responded to Zuckerberg's blitz with impressive packages of its own.\par \par \uc2 Not everyone on \uc2 Meta\uc2 's list gets $100 million, though they're still fetching sums that were previously unimaginable.\par \par \uc2 One of Zuckerberg's latest hires is a DeepMind alum and former \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  employee named Lucas Beyer. As a boy in Belgium, Beyer dreamed of making videogames and developed a fascination with AI. At college in Germany, he focused on mechanical engineering and dabbled in machine learning.\par \par \uc2 But when he applied for a job at \uc2 Google\uc2  as a software engineer more than a decade ago, he was rejected and decided to pursue a Ph.D.\par \par \uc2 At first, he studied quantum physics and learned something useful. "Quantum physics is just not my thing," Beyer said on the AI Epiphany show. He made computer vision and robotic perception his thing instead. When he hit the job market in 2018, he'd done two summer internships at \uc2 Google\uc2  and, this time, the company offered him a job.\par \par \uc2 In fact, his talents were in such high demand that he received offers from all the industry's top AI destinations -- except one.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Meta\uc2 ," he said on that show. "I never heard back."\par \par \uc2 These days, Beyer is hearing directly from \uc2 Meta\uc2 's CEO.\par \par \uc2 After six years as a staff research scientist at Google Brain and DeepMind, Beyer left in 2024 to start \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's office in Zurich with two colleagues, Alexander Kolesnikov and Xiaohua Zhai.\par \par \uc2 Now they're on the move again -- because Zuckerberg just hired all three of them.\par \par \uc2 "Yes, we will be joining \uc2 Meta\uc2 ," Beyer wrote on X this week. "No, we did not get 100M."\par \par \uc2 There are some clues that help executives and recruiters assess talent. They can poke around Google Scholar and browse through the papers that researchers have published, checking to see how often their work has been cited.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg himself has been spending his days weeding through wonky papers, searching for hotshot engineers and scientists to recruit for his Superintelligence team of roughly 50 people. He's in a group chat with two \uc2 Meta\uc2  executives called "Recruiting Party" in which they discuss hundreds of potential candidates and tactics for approaching them -- like whether they prefer to be contacted by email, text or WhatsApp.\par \par \uc2 The people who get notes from Zuckerberg have a few things in common. They need to know calculus, linear algebra and probability theory, like one of \uc2 Meta\uc2 's recent hires, who says he's fascinated by algorithm design.\par \par \uc2 An undergraduate degree in computer science isn't nearly enough training. These researchers get Ph.D.s from elite programs -- Berkeley, Stanford, Carnegie Mellon, MIT -- whose most selective fields admit less than 1% of applicants and have become a feeder system to AI labs.\par \par \uc2 Today's researchers also had perfect timing. When many embarked on their Ph.D. work a decade ago, they were tackling esoteric topics in robotics and generative artificial intelligence. There was nothing remotely sexy about their fields. But they were exploring the frontier of AI -- and their areas of interest have yielded revolutionary advances.\par \par \uc2 Zhang, a researcher at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  who works on speech, was finishing up his first internship more than a decade ago when his mentor offered him some practical advice.\par \par \uc2 "You should not work on speech," Zhang was told, he recounted to \uc2 Carnegie Mellon\uc2  students last year. "This is a dead field." That mentor soon left for \uc2 Yahoo\uc2  to work on ads.\par \par \uc2 A few months later, the seemingly dead field was resuscitated by advances in deep learning, which created a new way for computers to analyze and understand speech patterns. Now, Zhang is the sort of engineer that every lab covets -- and Zuckerberg has reached out to him.\par \par \uc2 One reason all these companies are willing to shower recruits with cash is that even a superteam of AI engineers costs only a fraction of the price of AI infrastructure like data centers.\par \par \uc2 This year alone, \uc2 Meta\uc2  plans to invest around $70 billion on AI, while \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  are spending even more. Next to hardware, humans look like a bargain.\par \par \uc2 People in the tight-knit AI community say the researchers whose skills have never been so lucrative are not primarily motivated by huge piles of money.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, many were perfectly happy to become professors. These days, they're becoming researchers at AI labs -- but not just because of the pay. It's because only tech companies with seemingly limitless resources offer the computing power, data, infrastructure and freedom they need to run their experiments and scale their models.\par \par \uc2 "For my students and postdocs, the objective was never to do hot things and become a millionaire," said Alexei Efros, a \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , professor whose recent Ph.D. students include research scientists at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , DeepMind and \uc2 Anthropic\uc2 . "The objective is to try to solve cool, interesting, important, unsolved problems."\par \par \uc2 Those problems require money and chips. \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  have both in abundance.\par \par \uc2 But to win recruiting battles, top executives also have to get involved themselves.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO \uc2 Sam Altman\uc2  has invited potential hires to play poker or have dinner with him at his Russian Hill mansion in San Francisco, while President \uc2 Greg Brockman\uc2  used to host "Game of Thrones" watch parties. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  once hosted a recruiting party at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's former headquarters to entice researchers into joining \uc2 xAI\uc2 . At \uc2 Google\uc2 , CEO \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2  and co-founder \uc2 Sergey Brin\uc2  carve out time to bring over important hires.\par \par \uc2 The eight- and sometimes nine-figure pay packages are tempting, and the outreach from Zuckerberg is flattering, but \uc2 Meta\uc2 's laggard history in generative AI has made some recruits hesitant. When the company released its latest model to little fanfare, some researchers at \uc2 Meta\uc2  distanced themselves from the model, removing the project, called Llama 4, from their \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  bios.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg hasn't had much success in his efforts to hire the field's biggest stars, including \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's co-founder Ilya Sutskever and its chief research officer, Mark Chen.\par \par \uc2 Many candidates are happy to take a meeting at Zuckerberg's homes in Palo Alto and Lake Tahoe. In private, they are comparing gossip and calculating \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chances of winning the AI race.\par \par \uc2 The handful of researchers who are smartest about AI have built up what one described as "tribal knowledge" that is almost impossible to replicate. Rival researchers have lived in the same group houses in San Francisco, where they discuss papers that might provide clues for achieving the next great breakthrough.\par \par \uc2 When a Ph.D. student at Berkeley named Bill Peebles submitted his dissertation in 2023, he thanked another Ph.D. candidate named Tim Brooks.\par \par \uc2 "We are very aligned on research directions and interests," he wrote. "I hope we get to work on more stuff together in the future."\par \par \uc2 As it turned out, they would. Peebles and Brooks both went to work for \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and led the team that released its groundbreaking Sora text-to-video generator in 2024. Brooks has since left for DeepMind, while Peebles is still at \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  -- even after \uc2 Meta\uc2  recently tried to hire him.\par \par \uc2 And then Zuckerberg moved on to the next person on "The List."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich356\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal5340
