\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 FedEx\uc2  Outlook Subdued, Pacific Delivery Falls\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Hamilton \par \uc2 336 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 FedEx shared details about its European layoffs in June 2024. A Business News article on Wednesday about the package-shipping company incorrectly said FedEx would share details about the layoffs this month.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ June 28, 2025)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is expecting mixed results in its current first quarter as tariffs weigh on its international shipping business.\par \par \uc2 The package-shipping company said Tuesday it logged some of the biggest declines in shipping across the Pacific Ocean during the recently ended fourth quarter, as China-to-U.S. trade fell sharply after new tariffs were announced in April. \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is also cutting some of its European workforce as it continues cost-cutting efforts and looks to navigate uncertain customer demand in the year ahead.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot happening outside of \uc2 FedEx\uc2 . Trade policies are evolving and trade patterns are changing," Chief Executive \uc2 Raj Subramaniam\uc2  said during a call with analysts.\par \par \uc2 Shares fell 5% in after-hours trading.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  expects revenue to be flat or increase up to 2% in its fiscal first quarter. Analysts were anticipating a sales increase of about 0.6%. Earnings per share are projected to be $2.90 to $3.50, below the $3.83 a share analysts were forecasting.\par \par \uc2 The company didn't provide a full-year outlook, as it has in the past.\par \par \uc2 It is anticipating tariffs will add a $170 million cost in adjusted operating income during the current quarter, Chief Financial Officer \uc2 John Dietrich\uc2  said. International demand tracked tariff headlines during the quarter, falling in early April after President Trump's tariff announcements, and remained soft through May. Management expects international challenges will continue into the next fiscal year, particularly in China and across the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 "The macroeconomic environment remains uncertain," Dietrich said. "Our outlook is therefore based on current tariff rates, recent trends we're seeing, as well as that which we're hearing from our customers."\par \par \uc2 Subramaniam said \uc2 FedEx\uc2  is evaluating trade patterns and prepared to alter routes and capacity commitments if demand shifts. That evaluation is focused on Asia, Latin America and Europe. He will share details later this month about the planned layoffs in Europe, which are expected to save $150 million annually.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250625el6p0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
