\par\par \uc2 The Property Report\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to Invest Around $54 Billion in U.K. --- Thousands of jobs to be added in plan to boost AI, expand warehouse network\b0\par\par\uc2 By Najat Kantouar \par \uc2 506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  plans to invest about $54 billion in the U.K. over the next three years, expanding its warehouse network and artificial-intelligence infrastructure to bulk up its e-commerce and cloud operations.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. tech company said Tuesday that its investment of 40 billion pounds, equivalent to $54.10 billion, would create thousands of jobs and contribute to the country's economy and AI ambitions. U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  called the move a vote of confidence in the country.\par \par \uc2 News of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's investment came shortly after the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  launched its industrial strategy, a 10-year plan that aims to attract investment in key sectors to drive economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  set out plans for multibillion-dollar investments in several parts of the U.S. and countries like Australia and Taiwan in recent weeks. Most of those outlays seek to bolster its data-center network to meet booming global demand for AI computing.\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., the company is also looking to expand the infrastructure that underpins its core e-commerce operations, by investing in building new warehouses, adding delivery stations and upgrading its transportation network.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it expects to create 2,000 jobs at a previously announced fulfillment center in Hull that is set to open this year, another 2,000 positions at a new facility in Northampton, and additional roles at new sites in the East Midlands. The investment will also result in additional jobs at delivery stations across the country, the company said.\par \par \uc2 The plan comes after \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2  last week outlined plans to reduce the company's workforce in the coming years due to the increasing use of AI. Jassy said the efficiency gains from using generative AI would result in cuts, but didn't specify how much the size of \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's workforce would be reduced.\par \par \uc2 The company currently employs more than 75,000 people in the U.K. and is a top 10 private-sector employer, it said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's U.K. investment plan includes part of the GBP 8 billion it earmarked last year for building, operating and maintaining data centers in the country through 2028, it added.\par \par \uc2 The company also plans to allocate funds to original TV and film productions and to redevelop the historic Bray Film Studios in Berkshire.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  is seeking to spur business investment in sectors like advanced manufacturing, defense, technology and the creative industries.\par \par \uc2 The number of foreign-direct-investment projects into the U.K. declined last year, similar to other leading European economies, but the country continues to be the top destination for tech investments, according to a report by \uc2 EY\uc2  published in May. The U.K.'s share of foreign-direct-investment projects in Europe fell to 15.9% last year from 17.3% five years before, according to \uc2 EY\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250625el6p0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
