\par\par \b \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  To Cut Workforce By Several Thousand\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 294 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  plans to lay off several thousand employees in the next few weeks, looking to thin out its ranks while continuing its costly push into artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 The reduction of thousands of jobs will affect its sales department and other teams, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 The number of cuts isn't yet final, and they are planned for around the beginning of \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's new fiscal year, which starts in July, they said.\par \par \uc2 The latest round of layoffs come on top of the roughly 6,000 roles the company eliminated in May across product- and software-developer roles around the world. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  earlier reported on the planned summer layoffs.\par \par \uc2 Companies ranging from retailers to pharmaceuticals have begun drawing up plans to consolidate positions, looking to do more with leaner staffs and relying on technology for additional tasks.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  CEO \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2  said the company planned to reduce its workforce in the coming years because increasing use of artificial intelligence will eliminate the need for certain roles.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  sells AI tools to other firms that are meant to automate some tasks and has been investing heavily in data centers. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  had about 228,000 employees at the end of its last fiscal year, in June 2024, with roughly 45,000 of them in sales and marketing.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  eliminated 10,000 jobs in January 2023, retrenching after a pandemic-driven hiring frenzy by many tech companies. The company also made cuts to its videogame division last year after closing its acquisition of \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We continue to focus on building high-performing teams and increasing our agility by reducing layers," \uc2 Amy Hood\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's chief financial officer, told investors and analysts in April.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250620el6k0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
