\par\par \b \uc2 Hackers Turn Tech Support Into New Threat\b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert McMillan \par \uc2 884 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hackers in recent months have disrupted retail sales in the U.K. and U.S. and stolen hundreds of millions of dollars from crypto holders by targeting the outsourced call centers that many U.S. corporations use to save costs.\par \par \uc2 The hacks are often meticulously researched and use a variety of techniques, but they have one thing in common: low-level workers who staff call centers and have access to the kind of sensitive information that criminals need to commit crimes.\par \par \uc2 The focus on outside call centers has allowed attackers to trick workers to get around so-called two-factor account authentication techniques that send codes by text to mobile phones. Those methods are commonly used to protect millions of bank and credit-card accounts, as well as a host of other online portals.\par \par \uc2 In attacks on U.K. retailers including \uc2 Marks & Spencer\uc2  and \uc2 Harrods\uc2 , the hackers typically impersonated high-level corporate executives and pressured tech-support workers to give them access to corporate networks, according to security researchers. This is the same technique that allowed hackers to gain access to \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2  systems in 2023.\par \par \uc2 At the cryptocurrency company \uc2 Coinbase Global\uc2 , they simply paid off call-center workers in India, \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  said. The attack might cost the company as much as $400 million because it has pledged to reimburse customers who have lost millions in the scam. The hackers stole data belonging to as many as 97,000 \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  customers, the company estimated.\par \par \uc2 "You're working with a low-paid labor market," said Isaac Schloss, chief product officer at Contact Center Compliance, a company that advises call-center users and customers. "These people are in a position of poverty more often than not. So if the right opportunity comes for the right person, people are willing to look the other way."\par \par \uc2 Josh Cooper-Duckett, director of investigations at Cryptoforensic Investigators, said he began hearing last fall from victims who had typically lost more than a million dollars to hackers. In almost every instance, it was the same scam, said Cooper-Duckett, who helps victims of cryptocurrency theft recover their losses.\par \par \uc2 The criminals would buy information that they could use to call up their victims and masquerade as legitimate \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  workers, Cooper-Duckett said.\par \par \uc2 They would know their victim's personal information, their account balance, the last four digits of their bank accounts, and sometimes would have a list of recent transactions. Armed with this data, they would persuade them to create new cryptocurrency wallets with encryption keys known to the hacker or log into phishing sites and quickly steal their cryptocurrency.\par \par \uc2 "Every other day a new case would come in, and it would be, 'I got called by \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 , and I lost all my money because it wasn't \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 ,'" Cooper-Duckett said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  hackers bribed customer-support agents working for \uc2 TaskUs\uc2  and other support-desk companies. Such call-center employees typically have access to sensitive customer information that allows them to confirm the identity of callers, \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The criminals would cast a wide net, reaching out to insiders through social media or chat accounts such as \uc2 Telegram\uc2  with offers of $2,500 for help from insiders, \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 In some countries, workers don't face legal consequences for involvement in cyber breaches, said Philip Martin, \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 's chief security officer. "We've seen relatively limited consequences, in those regions, for perpetrators," he said. Even when workers are fired from such outsourcing jobs, "It's a relatively straightforward thing for them to go get a new one," he said.\par \par \uc2 Computers at outsourced companies have controls to prevent employees from stealing data, \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  said. It is typically impossible for an employee to plug a USB thumb drive into a computer or take a computer home from work. So the hackers started by offering workers cash payments for screenshots of their computers and the customer information they contained. These payments could amount to thousands of dollars, the company said.\par \par \uc2 In other instances, the hackers used malicious software, sneaking data-scraping code into computers. That allowed them to collect data in bulk and store it.\par \par \uc2 The first step to pulling off such a scam was asking their call-center insiders to describe all of the software running on their computers. Soon, the hackers discovered that workers were running an extension in their Chrome browsers -- extra software added to the browser that prevented web ads.\par \par \uc2 The extension had a bug in it, and that vulnerability allowed the criminals to sneak their own code into call-center computers. Now they could collect data in bulk and store it on the internet.\par \par \uc2 TaskUs\uc2  said it stopped taking \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  calls at the Indore, India, call center where the employees were being bribed, laying off 226 workers. It also fired two workers who were engaged in fraud earlier this year. \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  said that its own employees, as well as those at other outsourcing companies they work with, were recruited but declined to name them.\par \par \uc2 Companies have spent billions of dollars trying to reduce cyber threats, but hackers continue to find new ways to exploit human vulnerabilities.\par \par \uc2 "Consistently, the human interaction has proven to be a weak link," said Michael McPherson, a senior vice president with the cybersecurity company \uc2 ReliaQuest\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250620el6k00022\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
