\par\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2  Expands Employee Buyouts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Blunt \par \uc2 338 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  on Tuesday extended voluntary buyout offers to U.S. employees in multiple divisions, one of a number of cost-cutting steps it's made to help fund billions of dollars in AI spending.\par \par \uc2 The offers were extended to employees within its search and advertising units, as well as research, engineering and other areas of the company. The company has for years been culling workers as it invests tens of billions of dollars to develop cutting-edge artificial intelligence models and systems.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, employees within the platforms and devices division, which includes work on Android, Chrome and Pixel products, were offered voluntary buyouts, as were others in human resources, legal and finance.\par \par \uc2 "Some of our teams introduced a voluntary exit program with severance for US-based Googlers, and several more are now offering the program to support our important work ahead," the company said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The buyout offers were earlier reported by tech news site The Information.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  is substantially ramping up capital spending as it races to compete with ChatGPT and other AI competitors that threaten to erode its search dominance. The company expects to spend about $75 billion on capital expenditures this year, compared with $52.5 billion in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The company completed its largest-ever round of layoffs in 2023, when it cut about 12,000 employees, or 6% of its workforce. Since then, it has cut jobs in smaller increments.\par \par \uc2 Last year, \uc2 Anat Ashkenazi\uc2 , finance chief of \uc2 Google\uc2  parent \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , said the company is "working to balance our investments in AI and other growth areas with the cost discipline needed to fund those activities."\par \par \uc2 To get ahead in the AI race, \uc2 Google\uc2  has been improving the capabilities of its own AI model and chatbot, known as Gemini, and adding AI features to many of its products. Last month, it overhauled its classic search engine with the U.S. introduction of "AI Mode," which answers search queries in a chatbot-style conversation with fewer links.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250611el6b0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
