\par\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Seeks to Invest in AI Startup --- Talks proceed as software giant struggles to build the technology\b0\par\par\uc2 By Berber Jin and Meghan Bobrowsky \par \uc2 593 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms\uc2  is in advanced talks to invest around $14 billion into \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  and hire the startup's chief executive, \uc2 Alexandr Wang\uc2 , to help lead its AI efforts, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 The deal would be one of the social-media giant's largest-ever outside investments and show the dramatic steps it is taking to revamp its AI work, which has suffered setbacks including the delayed release of a major new model.\par \par \uc2 As part of the proposed deal -- which is still being finalized -- other \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  employees would join \uc2 Meta\uc2  alongside Wang as part of a new effort to develop advanced AI called superintelligence.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  CEO \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  personally got involved in the recruitment for the superintelligence effort after the company struggled to develop its recent Llama models, including by reaching out to AI researchers directly on WhatsApp, according to a person familiar with his approach.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 Chris Cox\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief product officer, sent an internal memo to some employees saying that the company was finalizing details on its plans for AI leadership.\par \par \uc2 Tech giants have largely struggled to keep up with the pace of AI innovation, forcing them to look toward younger startups for fresh talent. Antitrust scrutiny in recent years has made it difficult for them to acquire companies, so they are instead resorting to large minority investments in startups that allow them to hire their key employees. Last year, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  all made similar deals for other AI startups.\par \par \uc2 Unlike those startups, however, \uc2 Scale AI\uc2  isn't a research lab focused on building its own large language models from scratch. The startup is instead a data-labeling company, managing over 100,000 contractors from across the world who label images, write sentences and type out stories that help AI chatbots learn how to think and speak like humans.\par \par \uc2 The startup has sold its services to \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2  and has more recently begun to build software products that help businesses create AI tools, though that is a smaller part of its business.\par \par \uc2 The investment would give Meta 49% of the company, people familiar with the deal said. The social-media giant and the startup have discussed an arrangement in which \uc2 Meta\uc2  would receive nonvoting shares.\par \par \uc2 Should those turn into voting shares, Wang would have control over them, one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 A portion of the invested cash would be given to Scale shareholders, who would retain their ownership in the startup, the person said. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  and other outlets previously reported details of the deal.\par \par \uc2 Tech companies big and small are racing to build AI models that can surpass human intelligence, a threshold that industry leaders believe will reshape how society and the economy are organized as well as lavish riches onto the technology's winners.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Meta\uc2  deployed AI on a massive scale in the past year, the company has struggled to release a more advanced version of its biggest Llama model, delaying it until the fall or later. Two other recently released models came under fire after it was revealed that \uc2 Meta\uc2  submitted different versions of the models to a third-party performance test than they delivered to the general public.\par \par \uc2 Last month, senior executives at the company were so frustrated about the performance of the Llama team that they were considering making significant management changes to the group, The Wall Street Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250611el6b0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
