\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Unions Fight Trump's Immigration Plans --- Arrest of a top California leader helps galvanize organized labor\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan \par \uc2 779 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LOS ANGELES -- Unions, which have felt conflicted lately over how to deal with Trump, found an issue this past weekend over which to confront him: immigration.\par \par \uc2 It came after the head of the state's biggest union was arrested here for allegedly obstructing federal agents from conducting an immigration raid. The union says he was "peacefully observing." David Huerta, president of Service Employees International Union California, appeared in court Monday afternoon. He was released on $50,000 bail.\par \par \uc2 Just a half-mile away, his union, which represents a swath of working-class California, including nurses, janitors and security guards, organized a sprawling protest in downtown's Grand Park. Other unions declared solidarity with the \uc2 SEIU\uc2  and with immigrants caught up in federal raids.\par \par \uc2 The clash may become a watershed in how organized labor deals with Trump. In recent years, many rank-and-file union members warmed to Trump's outreach to working-class voters. Union leaders, who traditionally back Democrats, have wavered over how stringently to oppose him, if at all. Their decision to fight him in this instance in no small part reflects the growing presence of immigrants in union ranks.\par \par \uc2 As of Monday, the regional branches of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , though not their national leaders, and the national \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  had expressed solidarity with \uc2 SEIU\uc2 . Members of other local unions, including one that represents hotel and airport workers, showed up at the protest. Many said they saw labor and immigration as one fight.\par \par \uc2 "The struggle is very intertwined," said Maddisen Arguijo, 34 years old, a flight attendant who is a member of a different union for flight attendants and was at the park Monday.\par \par \uc2 Immigrants in Los Angeles "do essential work that we need, that we cannot do without," she said. "To pretend like that is not the truth of the matter and demoralize them and act like they are criminals and all these insane things that are being said in the media, it's a fallacy."\par \par \uc2 At Monday's protest, many wore purple \uc2 SEIU\uc2  shirts. Latin music played in the background. Protesters chanted "Whose streets? Our streets!" One \uc2 SEIU\uc2  leader called out "Todos somos Huerta," or, "we are all Huerta." Members of another large local union, \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , which represents hotel and airport workers, arrived banging drums and chanting for justice.\par \par \uc2 Immigrants made up one-third of California's labor force in 2023, according to census data, compared with 18% for the overall U.S. (The data doesn't distinguish by legal status.)\par \par \uc2 Some of unions' biggest organizing wins recently have been in service workplaces where undocumented immigrants often make up an outsize share.\par \par \uc2 "Whether you're documented or not, you can be a member of a union, and those we represent, we vow to protect," said Steve Smith, a spokesman for the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Alfred Munoz, an Amazon delivery driver, said he joined the protest Monday because Huerta and the \uc2 SEIU\uc2  have supported the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 ' effort to organize his workplace. Huerta sent \uc2 SEIU\uc2  members to join a picket line during a multiday strike at Amazon in December, said Munoz, 43.\par \par \uc2 "If they can support us, then why can't we support them?" Munoz said. He added that he has family members who are immigrants and are concerned about Trump's policies. "You never know when ICE is going to come, and that's a messed-up feeling."\par \par \uc2 One reason the raids by \uc2 Immigration and Customs Enforcement\uc2  have drawn such protests is that Los Angeles is home to precisely the sort of undocumented immigrants that Americans, according to polls, don't think should be targeted: long-established residents who pay taxes and have never committed a crime.\par \par \uc2 Most of California's unauthorized immigrants arrived during the 1980s, 1990s and early 2000s, when Mexico's economy was in crisis and bloody civil wars gripped parts of Central America.\par \par \uc2 In the Los Angeles metropolitan area, 72% of Hispanic immigrants with a high-school education or less -- a group that includes many undocumented immigrants -- have been in the country for more than 20 years, compared with 54% nationwide and 43% in Florida, according to 2023 census data aggregated by IPUMS at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 During much of the 20th century, union leaders saw immigrants as a threat to their members' livelihoods. But as declining manufacturing thinned their traditional base, union organizers looked to other sectors for new members. Los Angeles was one of the first places where the shift took hold, starting with an \uc2 SEIU\uc2  campaign to organize janitorial workers in the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250610el6a0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
