\par\par \b \uc2 High Court Bolsters Workers' Reverse Bias Suits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jan Wolfe and Erin Mulvaney \par \uc2 830 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court made it easier for workers to sue for reverse discrimination in a unanimous ruling that allowed a woman to pursue a claim that she was denied a job promotion because she is straight.\par \par \uc2 The decision on Thursday revives a lawsuit brought by Marlean Ames, who alleged the Ohio state agency where she works denied her a promotion, and then demoted her, because she is heterosexual. She said that both her old job and the one she had sought were given to gay people. Her supervisor at the time was also gay.\par \par \uc2 The high court, in an opinion by Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , said a Cincinnati-based appeals court erred by requiring Ames to show "background circumstances" suggesting the employer was hostile to straight people -- which wouldn't have been required had a gay employee claimed discrimination.\par \par \uc2 The ruling resolves a split that had emerged among federal appeals courts over whether the "background circumstances" test should be applied to reverse discrimination claims brought by individuals from majority demographic groups, such as straight or white people. Five federal appeals courts use that standard, while three appeals courts have rejected it.\par \par \uc2 Jackson said that adding more legal hurdles for plaintiffs who aren't minorities "cannot be squared with the text of Title VII [of the Civil Rights Act of 1964] or our longstanding precedents."\par \par \uc2 Title VII prohibits discrimination because of an "individual's race, color, religion, sex, or national origin." The Supreme Court extended that protection to LGBT workers in 2020. But public opinion is divided over whether equal opportunity should require absolute neutrality, or if it should take into account the historical discrimination that minority groups have faced.\par \par \uc2 Claims of discrimination from members of majority groups have already become more common in recent years, and Gibson Dunn partner Jason Schwartz said Thursday's decision "will supercharge the current wave of reverse discrimination lawsuits by removing a significant obstacle for plaintiffs."\par \par \uc2 Ames was represented at the high court by the University of Virginia School of Law's Supreme Court Litigation Clinic.\par \par \uc2 Xiao Wang, the clinic's director, said the court's decision makes clear that "Title VII is supposed to protect everyone on the same terms."\par \par \uc2 "I think people just want to make sure that whatever discrimination looks like, it needs to be subject to the same standards," Wang said.\par \par \uc2 Ames's case will now return to the lower court. Dominic Binkley, a spokesman for Ohio Attorney General \uc2 Dave Yost\uc2 , said the state agency where Ames worked didn't unlawfully discriminate against her.\par \par \uc2 Ames has worked since 2004 at the \uc2 Ohio Department of Youth Services\uc2 , which runs the state's juvenile correctional facilities. She was in charge of the agency's prison-rape prevention program in 2019 when she applied for a promotion.\par \par \uc2 Ames didn't get the job and, according to her suit, a department supervisor suggested she consider retirement. The following month, Ames was stripped of her current post and offered the choice of demotion to a lower-paying position or leaving the department altogether.\par \par \uc2 A lesbian received the promotion Ames had sought, and administrators gave her old prison-rape prevention job to a 25-year-old gay man. The state said she was reassigned not due to discrimination but because of a reorganization in the department.\par \par \uc2 Ohio argued that the legal standards applied by the lower courts had been misunderstood and that members of majority groups, like Ames, didn't really face higher hurdles in court.\par \par \uc2 The court rejected that argument, saying the rule effectively required members of majority groups to produce evidence of discrimination, such as statistical proof, that would not be required from minorities.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NAACP Legal Defense and Educational Fund\uc2  and other civil-rights groups submitted a brief in support of the state, arguing it was still proper "for courts to consider the reality that, historically and presently, employment discrimination has been far more likely to occur against minority-group members than majority-group members."\par \par \uc2 Ames got backing from critics of diversity, equity and inclusion -- or DEI -- programs. Among the groups filing briefs in support of her was America First Legal Foundation, a legal group founded by White House aide \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , which has filed civil-rights complaints against large employers over their hiring practices.\par \par \uc2 The backlash to DEI programs was stoked by the high court's 2023 decision ending the use of race in college admissions. The decision in cases filed against Harvard College and the University of North Carolina spurred many private companies to roll back their DEI programs that they had championed only years before, even though they weren't directly implicated in the ruling. The Trump administration has also sought to dismantle DEI programs.\par \par \uc2 An analysis by Gibson Dunn showed a steady increase in lawsuits challenging DEI programs since the Harvard decision. Major companies that have been sued include \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , Meta, \uc2 Expedia\uc2 , \uc2 CBS\uc2  and \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250606el660003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
