\par\par \uc2 Technology & Media\par \b \uc2 AI Strength Limits Tariff Impact on \uc2 TSMC\uc2  --- Company's clients, such as \uc2 Apple\uc2 , have found supply chains increasingly strained\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yang Jie and Joyu Wang \par \uc2 660 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co.\uc2  faces limited impact from tariffs, its chief executive said, reaffirming that artificial-intelligence demand remains strong, and the company guided for record earnings.\par \par \uc2 At an annual shareholders' meeting on Tuesday, Chief Executive \uc2 C.C. Wei\uc2  said tariffs are typically borne by importers and won't directly affect \uc2 TSMC\uc2 , the world's largest contract chip maker.\par \par \uc2 Still, he warned that if tariffs lead to a global economic downturn and higher prices, that would damp demand, indirectly affecting \uc2 TSMC\uc2 's business.\par \par \uc2 Top \uc2 TSMC\uc2  clients, including \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , have found their supply chains increasingly strained amid widespread uncertainty over President Trump's trade policies. Despite this, Wei reiterated confidence in the strength of AI-driven semiconductor demand.\par \par \uc2 He expects \uc2 TSMC\uc2 's revenue and earnings to reach new record highs this year.\par \par \uc2 According to Taiwan-based research firm DigiTimes, \uc2 TSMC\uc2  and Taiwan's broader foundry industry have seen robust sales growth so far in the April-to-June quarter. That's thanks to continued demand for AI and high-performance computing chips, a rebound in smartphone application processor shipments, and customers' push to build up inventory to get ahead of tariffs, it said in a report Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "AI will be something you absolutely can't live without in the future," Wei told reporters after the meeting. "As long as there's demand for AI, \uc2 TSMC\uc2  will always be a great company with a really promising outlook."\par \par \uc2 Wei also addressed concerns over worker safety at the company's plants and technology theft.\par \par \uc2 A dozen protesters holding a white banner stood outside the event in \uc2 TSMC\uc2 's home base of Hsinchu, Taiwan, protesting a string of incidents since 2019 that resulted in deaths and injuries to workers.\par \par \uc2 "Life is precious, and public safety is paramount to us," Wei said. "We recognize areas where we fell short, and I sincerely apologize on behalf of the company."\par \par \uc2 The CEO firmly dismissed shareholders' worries about technology being stolen from \uc2 TSMC\uc2 's overseas operations as it expands abroad. Emphasizing robust safeguards and the complexity of its technological capabilities, he said of \uc2 TSMC\uc2 's intellectual property: "It cannot be stolen."\par \par \uc2 Wei elaborated that \uc2 TSMC\uc2 's cutting-edge tech is the product of over 10,000 research and development engineers, followed by another large team of production engineers who optimize and refine it.\par \par \uc2 "If our technology could be stolen that easily, \uc2 TSMC\uc2  wouldn't be where it is today," he said.\par \par \uc2 The CEO said new technologies are first implemented in a so-called mother fab in Taiwan before reaching the volume production stage. This involves deploying hundreds of engineers to \uc2 TSMC\uc2 's headquarters for training, and another two to three hundred workers to set up production lines abroad.\par \par \uc2 "The technologies have progressed to a point where it's beyond the capacity of an individual, 10 people, or even a hundred, to steal," he said.\par \par \uc2 Wei also denied reports that \uc2 TSMC\uc2  is planning to build new chip-fabrication facilities in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 "Do you think it's possible to have customers in the Middle East? I mean, do you really think that could happen? From what I can see, it's not that easy to build up a semiconductor industry there," he said.\par \par \uc2 Even in an established location like Taiwan, building chip-making facilities requires at least two-and-half years, Wei said.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the U.S. market, Wei said that a shortage of experienced labor in Arizona, where \uc2 TSMC\uc2  has manufacturing operations, makes it challenging to ramp up production capacity on American soil.\par \par \uc2 He said Trump had asked if \uc2 TSMC\uc2  can complete its announced $100 billion investment in the U.S. within five years or so. Wei said he had told the U.S. president that this would be very difficult, to which Trump replied: "Mr. Wei, do your best."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich445\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal6675
