\par\par \b \uc2 Warner CEO's Pay Plan Rejected --- Shareholders say no to a $51.9 million package for Zaslav in a symbolic vote\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Flint \par \uc2 447 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  shareholders rejected the multimillion-dollar pay packages of Chief Executive \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2  and his leadership team.\par \par \uc2 More than 59% of Warner shareholders who cast ballots voted against the compensation Zaslav and his team received for 2024, the company said Tuesday in a securities filing. More than 80% of Warner shareholders voted on the executive compensation, including Zaslav's $51.9 million package.\par \par \uc2 The rejection is a symbolic rebuke of the Warner leadership's pay packages, since the shareholders' "say on pay" vote is nonbinding.\par \par \uc2 The stock has fallen about 59% since the company was created from the merger of \uc2 AT&T\uc2 's \uc2 Warner Media\uc2  and Discovery Communications in April 2022.\par \par \uc2 The company's board said it "takes the results of the annual advisory vote on executive compensation seriously" and that the board's compensation committee "looks forward to continuing its regular practice of engaging in constructive dialogue with our shareholders."\par \par \uc2 Shareholder concern about executive compensation at Warner Discovery has been growing. Last year, 50.8% of shareholders approved of the executive compensation at the company. Anything less than 70% is seen as "low support" by \uc2 ISS\uc2 , a corporate-governance firm.\par \par \uc2 Like many media companies, Warner has struggled in recent years as its traditional cable businesses lose ground to streaming. Consumers have been moving away from traditional cable and satellite television. Warner cable networks, including the Food Network, Discovery and CNN, are enduring rating and revenue declines.\par \par \uc2 S&P Global Ratings\uc2  recently downgraded Warner debt to junk status primarily due to the challenges facing its cable-network business.\par \par \uc2 Warner's streaming service HBO Max is profitable and is adding subscribers, but its subscriber count isn't close to the reach of \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 + and other streaming services.\par \par \uc2 The company is also exploring a potential spinoff of its cable networks. Last December the company said it was restructuring into two operating divisions -- one focused on its streaming and movie and television production studios and the other on its cable networks.\par \par \uc2 Entertainment chieftains often have higher compensation than chief executives in other industries.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  Chief Executives \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2  and \uc2 Greg Peters\uc2  each had compensation packages valued at more than $60 million in 2024. \uc2 Disney\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2  had a pay package valued at $41.1 million.\par \par \uc2 Shareholders at other entertainment companies have also expressed disapproval of the compensation, in nonbinding votes.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  shareholders voted against the pay compensation of its top executives including Sarandos, Peters and Chairman \uc2 Reed Hastings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In 2018, \uc2 Disney\uc2  shareholders voted against Iger's compensation plan.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250604el640000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
