\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: FEMA Is Going Back To Old Hurricane Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Scott Patterson and Tarini Parti \par \uc2 338 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2  officials are scrapping a hurricane-response plan that its recently appointed leader, David Richardson, had said was close to completion, according to agency staff.\par \par \uc2 With hurricane season kicking off this month, Richardson told staff Monday the agency would return to the same guidance for hurricane response as last year. Some were confused how, given the agency had eliminated key programs and sharply cut its workforce.\par \par \uc2 Richardson told FEMA staff in a Monday call that he didn't want to create a new plan that might contradict what a newly created FEMA review council, co-chaired by Homeland Security Secretary \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , might propose.\par \par \uc2 The new FEMA leader suggested he recently learned there is an annual hurricane season, stunning the workforce of the agency tasked with responding to disasters. He has expressed surprise in meetings at the scope of the agency's mission, The Wall Street Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 "Yesterday, as everybody knows, [was the] first day of hurricane season," Richardson said. "I didn't realize it was a season." The season lasts from June 1 through November.\par \par \uc2 "Despite mean-spirited attempts to falsely frame a joke as policy, there is no uncertainty about what FEMA will be doing this Hurricane Season," said a FEMA representative, adding, "FEMA is laser-focused on disaster response, and protecting the American people."\par \par \uc2 The agency has been plagued by high-level departures. MaryAnn Tierney, until recently the acting No. 2, resigned in late May. A longtimer at FEMA, she told staff "the current approach lacks a clear end state or plan and has been done recklessly without regard to our current statutory or moral obligations to the American people," according to a copy of the message seen by the Journal.\par \par \uc2 FEMA's leadership has been trying to balance White House pressure to make significant changes toward diminishing the agency's role with making sure of adequate responses to disasters, staffers say.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250603el630000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
