\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Markets & Finance: \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Quits Smaller American Businesses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Wallace \par \uc2 450 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is disbanding a division that serves small and medium-size companies in the U.S., a spokeswoman said, part of a broader drive to focus on markets where it has an edge.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  laid off 40 employees in the division, which is known as business banking. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  also announced the decision to the business bank's roughly 4,400 clients, firms that have up to $50 million in revenue.\par \par \uc2 "We are supporting . . . clients while they transition to a suitable alternative provider," the spokeswoman said. The bank will retain some clients, managed by different teams, she added.\par \par \uc2 Most of the clients \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is letting go are domestic, rather than U.S. subsidiaries of international companies.\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive \uc2 Georges Elhedery\uc2  has been overhauling \uc2 HSBC\uc2  since taking the helm last fall. He wants to boost profits by removing duplicated roles and funneling resources into areas where the bank plays in the big league.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , listed in London, is Europe's biggest bank by assets and plays a key role in the world economy by financing trade and processing transactions. Even before Elhedery took over, however, it was retreating from parts of the world where it once sought to compete.\par \par \uc2 The bank is prioritizing its home markets, Hong Kong and the U.K., and sharpening its focus on Asia.\par \par \uc2 This past week's pullback on Main Street follows hot on the heels of a retreat on Wall Street. Earlier in the year, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said it planned to stop advising companies on doing deals and going public in the U.S., as well as Europe.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  largely quit retail banking in the U.S. in 2021, when it agreed to sell parts of that business to Citizens Bank and \uc2 Cathay Bank\uc2 . The next year, it struck a deal to sell its Canadian operations to \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 North America accounted for about 3% of \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's pretax profit in 2024, down from around 6% five years earlier. Over the same period, employees in North America fell from almost 17,000 to fewer than 7,000.\par \par \uc2 Other European banks, such as \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , have also abandoned efforts to compete in Main Street banking in the highly competitive U.S. market in recent years.\par \par \uc2 In one area, though, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is bulking up in the U.S. Having rescued \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 's U.K. subsidiary in 2023, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is using that as a launchpad to do more business with startups around the world including in Boston, New York and San Francisco. It retains a wealth division in the U.S. for rich clients, and a wholesale bank for big corporate customers.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250531el5v0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
