\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Trump Doubles Tariff on Steel, Aluminum --- At a Pittsburgh-area rally to tout Nippon-U.S. Steel deal, he says levy will hit 50%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kris Maher and Bob Tita \par \uc2 954 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2025\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEST MIFFLIN, Pa. -- President Trump said he would double tariffs on imported steel, a move he said would bolster the domestic industry and protect U.S. jobs.\par \par \uc2 Trump announced the duties at a Friday evening rally near Pittsburgh promoting a $14 billion deal between Tokyo-based \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  and \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 , which he said would ensure U.S. control over the storied steelmaker.\par \par \uc2 "There's never been a $14 billion investment in the history of the steel industry in the United States," Trump said. "Most importantly, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  will continue to be controlled by the U.S.A."\par \par \uc2 Trump said tariffs on imported steel, as well as aluminum, would increase to 50% from the current 25%, effective Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Global prices for steel have been falling in recent months, making it easier for buyers to pay the existing duty on imports and still acquire steel at a discount to domestic prices.\par \par \uc2 Trump has hailed the planned deal between \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  and \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  as a "partnership." For a year and a half, the Tokyo-based steelmaker has been pursuing a $14 billion takeover of its Pittsburgh-based counterpart, in recent months pledging additional investment in the company's plants to help win Trump's backing.\par \par \uc2 Many details remain unclear about a deal that could reshape the American steel sector. Since last week, the companies have been tight-lipped about the next steps.\par \par \uc2 Federal oversight of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  is expected to be part of a national-security agreement between the government and \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 . The company has offered to establish a separate board to oversee its North American business, The Wall Street Journal has reported.\par \par \uc2 U.S. citizens would make up a majority of the board, and top managers would also be Americans. A federal monitor would be appointed to oversee compliance with the national-security agreement under the proposed arrangement.\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  had sought full ownership, saying that its investment in technology and equipment upgrades wouldn't be justified otherwise.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 David Burritt\uc2  and \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  Vice Chairman \uc2 Takahiro Mori\uc2  took the stage before Trump and said the deal wouldn't have happened without his intervention.\par \par \uc2 "You have placed your trust in us, and we will do our part in the years ahead to earn that trust," Mori said. "Now, we will start to make the massive investments that will transform \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "Because of him, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  stays mined, melted and made in America," Burritt said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  shareholders have said they haven't received notice of any changes to the terms of \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's $14.1 billion offer, suggesting to some that the original deal approved by shareholders last April will be completed.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 , which employs 14,000 workers in North America, will keep its headquarters in Pittsburgh and retain its name, Trump has said, describing \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  as a "partial owner."\par \par \uc2 Trump said Friday that Nippon would invest $2.2 billion in \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 's Mon Valley Works outside Pittsburgh, and $7 billion in Indiana, Alabama, Arkansas and Minnesota. Under the deal, the company's blast furnaces would operate for the next 10 years and workers would get a $5,000 bonus.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 , Trump's Friday rally is set to mark a reversal from January, when former President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  blocked the deal on national-security grounds. Trump, too, had opposed the sale on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 After starting his second term, he restarted talks with \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  executives, with an emphasis on increasing the company's investment commitments beyond the $2.7 billion it had pledged. Trump ordered a new national security review of the deal in April.\par \par \uc2 Leadership at the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2 , the union that represents \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  employees, has strongly opposed a sale. The union said Friday it remained skeptical, because of a lack of details about investments and clarity on whether \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  was buying the entire company.\par \par \uc2 "Whatever the deal structure, our primary concern remains with the impact that this merger of \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  into a foreign competitor will have on national security, our members and the communities where we live and work," said \uc2 David McCall\uc2 , president of the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  has said it would not interfere with U.S. trade policies and wouldn't import steel from its Asian mills as a substitute for \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2 -produced steel.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  workers have fretted about diminishing investment in older mills, especially the three that make up the Mon Valley Works. \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  had threatened to halt production there if it couldn't sell to \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 , saying it couldn't afford needed upgrades.\par \par \uc2 Kim Beveridge, 64 years old, a \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  mobile crane operator at the rally, said those threats and the cancellation of more than $1 billion of planned improvements in 2021 were like a "slap in the face." He said he welcomed \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2 's promised investments.\par \par \uc2 Beveridge said he worried about the lack of details but that he trusted Trump's business acumen and believed the deal would benefit workers.\par \par \uc2 Chris Kelly, the mayor of West Mifflin, Pa. -- home to the Irvin Works, the site of Friday's rally -- said everyone he was talking to locally was upbeat about the news, despite not knowing the full details.\par \par \uc2 "I'm taking it as positive," he said. "They're having a big rally. They're not having a big rally to say we lost our jobs."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020250531el5v0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
